rationale": "The last deploy was 09:52 UTC, about 4 h 15 min before the 14:07 alert, so there was NO deploy in the prior 2 h. Conditioning on the 'no deploy' column (20 incidents): bad push 4/20 = 0.20, upstream 13/20 = 0.65, hardware 3/20 = 0.15. The on-call's 60 % is the unconditional base rate (30/50).", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "bad_push", "why_hard": "The base rate favours a bad push, and a deploy happened that day; you have to check that it falls outside the 2-hour window and condition on it."}, "question": {"criteria": {"bad_push": "A bad config/code push.", "database_hardware": "A database host or hardware fault.", "upstream_provider": "Slowness at the upstream payment provider."}, "instructions": "What is the most likely root cause of the new LAT-CHK alert? Give probabilities for each cause that reflect the reference data and the guidance.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "CLOUDMERE SRE — POSTMORTEM STATISTICS for alert pattern \"checkout p99 latency > 2 s for 10 min\" (pattern ID LAT-CHK)\n\nReference data: all 50 LAT-CHK incidents from 2024-01 to 2026-08 with a confirmed root cause, broken down by whether any production deploy to the checkout path happened in the 2 hours before the alert.\n\n| Confirmed root cause           | Incidents | …of which: deploy in prior 2 h | …of which: NO deploy in prior 2 h |\n|--------------------------------|----------:|-------------------------------:|----------------------------------:|\n| Bad config/code push           |        30 |                             26 |                                  4 |\n| Upstream payment-provider slowness |    15 |                              2 |                                 13 |\n| Database host / hardware fault |         5 |                              2 |                                  3 |\n| Total                          |        50 |                             30 |                                 20 |\n\nGuidance for the on-call: when a new LAT-CHK alert fires, estimate the root-cause probabilities from this table, conditioning on every attribute of the new alert that the table breaks down by. Assume the new incident is drawn from the same population.\n\nNEW ALERT — 2026-09-19 14:07 UTC, LAT-CHK firing.\nDeploy log, checkout path, 2026-09-19: last production deploy 09:52 UTC (checkout-web v4.18.2). No deploys since.\nOn-call first reaction in chat: \"LAT-CHK is almost always a bad push (60 % of the time). Let's roll back 4.18.2.\"\n"}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "probability", "group": "hard-opus-b-probability-03", "id": "hard-opus-b-probability-03", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 662, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "gold_probs": {"no": 0.3125, "yes": 0.6875}, "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "INV-26511 (EUR 18,420) is in the 'large' band (> 10,000). The list has 16 large invoices, 11 of them paid late: P(late) = 11/16 = 0.6875, P(on time) = 5/16 = 0.3125. The account manager's figure (22/35 on time) mixes in 19 small invoices, which were almost all paid on time.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "no", "why_hard": "The pooled record makes the customer look reliable; only counting the size band CC-4 prescribes (35 rows, amounts to be compared with 10,000) reverses the answer."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The invoice will be paid on or before its due date.", "true": "The invoice will be paid after its due date."}, "instructions": "Will invoice INV-26511 be paid after its due date? Give probabilities that reflect the evidence under rule CC-4.", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "HOLLIS & PARK DISTRIBUTION — CREDIT CONTROL NOTE\nCustomer: Meridian Hotels Group (account 30-7781), payment terms 30 days net.\n\nCredit-control forecasting rule CC-4: to forecast whether an open invoice will be paid late, use this customer's invoices settled in the last 12 months that fall in the same size band as the open invoice. Size bands: \"small\" = up to EUR 10,000; \"large\" = above EUR 10,000 (Meridian routes large invoices through a second approval step at head office). The probability of late payment is the share of those invoices that were paid after the due date.\n\nInvoices settled Sep 2025 – Aug 2026 (complete list, amounts in EUR):\n| Invoice    | Amount  | Settlement |\n|------------|--------:|------------|\n| INV-25300 |  37,469 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25301 |  47,435 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25302 |   9,611 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25303 |  30,217 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25304 |  46,482 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25305 |  17,720 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25306 |   6,527 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25307 |   4,970 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25308 |   6,824 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25309 |   2,241 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25310 |   6,046 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25311 |   8,528 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25312 |  34,406 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25313 |   5,819 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25314 |  46,718 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25315 |  16,754 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25316 |   9,033 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25317 |   5,811 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25318 |   9,011 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25319 |   1,876 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25320 |  28,980 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25321 |   7,905 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25322 |  18,728 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25323 |   4,899 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25324 |  16,386 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25325 |   9,504 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25326 |  21,845 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25327 |   8,324 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25328 |  45,435 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25329 |  22,313 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25330 |   1,928 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25331 |  47,416 | paid after due date |\n| INV-25332 |  19,454 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25333 |   4,274 | paid on or before due date |\n| INV-25334 |   3,845 | paid on or before due date |\n\nOpen invoice: INV-26511, EUR 18,420.00, due 2026-10-09.\nAccount manager: \"Meridian is one of our most reliable payers — 22 of 35 invoices on time last year.\"\n"}
{"expected": "attended", "family": "probability", "group": "hard-opus-b-probability-04", "id": "hard-opus-b-probability-04", "labels": ["no_show", "late_cancellation", "attended"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 371, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "gold_probs": {"attended": 0.8, "late_cancellation": 0.12, "no_show": 0.08}, "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The 24-hour mark is Sun 2026-09-20 09:40. The patient's 'Y' arrived at 08:15 that day, before the mark, so the status at the mark is 'Confirmed by patient'. That row gives attended 160/200 = 0.80, no-show 16/200 = 0.08, late cancellation 24/200 = 0.12. The receptionist's 21 % is the all-appointments no-show rate (210/1000).", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "no_show", "why_hard": "Requires checking that the reply came before the 24-hour cut-off and using the confirmed row, not the pooled rates or the 'delivered, no reply' row."}, "question": {"criteria": {"attended": "The patient attends the appointment.", "late_cancellation": "The patient cancels less than 24 h before the appointment.", "no_show": "The patient does not attend and does not cancel."}, "instructions": "Estimate the outcome of appointment EP-50221 on 2026-09-21 09:40. Give probabilities for each outcome that reflect the statistics under the scheduling-aid rule.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "ELMBROOK FAMILY PRACTICE (fictional) — FRONT-DESK SCHEDULING AID\nAppointment outcome statistics, Elmbrook main site, adult appointments, Jan–Aug 2026 (all 1,000 appointments with a recorded outcome):\n\n| Reminder status at 24 h before | Appointments | Attended | No-show | Late cancellation (< 24 h) |\n|--------------------------------|-------------:|---------:|--------:|---------------------------:|\n| Confirmed by patient (SMS \"Y\") |          200 |      160 |      16 |                         24 |\n| Reminder delivered, no reply   |          500 |      340 |     110 |                         50 |\n| Reminder not delivered         |          300 |      180 |      84 |                         36 |\n| All                            |        1,000 |      680 |     210 |                        110 |\n\nScheduling-aid rule: to estimate a booked appointment's outcome, use the row matching the appointment's reminder status at the 24-hour mark; the outcome probabilities are that row's relative frequencies. If the status changes after the 24-hour mark (e.g. a late reply), it is ignored for this estimate.\n\nAppointment to estimate: Mon 2026-09-21 09:40, patient ID EP-50221, adult, main site.\nReminder log (24-hour mark for this appointment: Sun 2026-09-20 09:40):\n  - SMS sent Sat 2026-09-19 09:40\n  - SMS delivery receipt: DELIVERED Sat 2026-09-19 09:41\n  - Patient reply \"Y\" received Sun 2026-09-20 08:15\nReceptionist: \"With the overall 21 % no-show rate I'd double-book that slot.\"\n"}
{"expected": "upheld", "family": "probability", "group": "hard-opus-b-probability-06", "id": "hard-opus-b-probability-06", "labels": ["overturned", "modified", "upheld"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 684, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "gold_probs": {"modified": 0.0625, "overturned": 0.25, "upheld": 0.6875}, "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "AP-7390 is harassment, filed exactly 7 days after the action (within 7 inclusive), no prior suspension. Comparable decided appeals: harassment, ≤ 7 days, prior suspension 'no' → n = 16: upheld 11, overturned 4, modified 1, so P = 11/16 = 0.6875, 4/16 = 0.25, 1/16 = 0.0625. Across all 30 rows overturned is 16/30, the source of the reviewer's impression.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "overturned", "why_hard": "The whole log is dominated by overturns; the three-way filter (including the inclusive 7-day boundary and excluding accounts with prior suspensions) flips the answer."}, "question": {"criteria": {"modified": "The removal stays but the strike is downgraded to a warning.", "overturned": "The content is restored.", "upheld": "The removal stays."}, "instructions": "Forecast the outcome of pending appeal AP-7390. Give probabilities for each outcome that reflect the evidence under rule AQ-3.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "LUMEN SOCIAL — APPEALS OPERATIONS, outcome forecast for queue prioritisation\n\nForecast rule AQ-3: estimate a pending appeal's outcome from decided appeals in the last quarter that are comparable. Comparable = same violated policy AND appeal filed within 7 days (inclusive) of the enforcement action AND same prior-suspension status (whether the account had a suspension before this enforcement). Use relative frequencies among comparable appeals.\n\nDecided appeals, Q2 2026 (complete sample drawn for this forecast):\n| Appeal  | Policy      | Filed after action | Prior suspension | Outcome    |\n|---------|-------------|--------------------|------------------|------------|\n| AP-7300 | harassment  |  6 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7301 | spam        |  4 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7302 | harassment  |  2 days | yes | upheld     |\n| AP-7303 | harassment  |  1 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7304 | harassment  |  6 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7305 | harassment  |  7 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7306 | harassment  |  9 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7307 | harassment  |  2 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7308 | harassment  |  6 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7309 | harassment  |  8 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7310 | harassment  |  4 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7311 | hate_speech |  3 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7312 | harassment  |  6 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7313 | hate_speech |  6 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7314 | spam        |  2 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7315 | harassment  |  5 days | no  | modified   |\n| AP-7316 | harassment  | 15 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7317 | harassment  | 12 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7318 | harassment  |  3 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7319 | harassment  |  1 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7320 | harassment  |  3 days | yes | upheld     |\n| AP-7321 | harassment  |  3 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7322 | spam        | 20 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7323 | harassment  |  3 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7324 | harassment  |  3 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7325 | harassment  |  2 days | no  | upheld     |\n| AP-7326 | spam        |  1 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7327 | nudity      |  5 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7328 | harassment  |  3 days | no  | overturned |\n| AP-7329 | nudity      |  1 days | no  | overturned |\n\nOutcome definitions: upheld = removal stays; overturned = content restored; modified = removal stays but strike downgraded to warning.\n\nPENDING APPEAL AP-7390: post removed under harassment policy on 2026-09-11; appeal filed 2026-09-18 (7 days after); account has no prior suspensions.\nQueue note from a reviewer: \"Appeals get overturned more often than not these days — prioritise this one as a likely restore.\"\n"}
{"expected": "escalate_to_engineering", "family": "tradeoff", "group": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-01", "id": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-01", "labels": ["escalate_to_security", "page_oncall", "escalate_to_engineering", "process_refund", "send_kb_article"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 633, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "tn_9120 is the customer's own 'EU Subsidiary' workspace on the same Org ID ORG-5512, and SP-4 §2 says workspaces sharing an Org ID are the same organization, so R1 (cross-organization exposure) does not apply. R2 does not apply (37 users active, status green). The failing export has a click path (Reports > Q3 Pipeline > Export > CSV, EXP-409) from an Enterprise org, so R3 is the highest-ranked applicable rule; the credit request (R4) waits until R3 is resolved.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "escalate_to_security", "why_hard": "The customer loudly claims someone else's data is in the file; only the definition of 'organization' plus the workspace list shows it is their own subsidiary workspace."}, "question": {"criteria": {"escalate_to_engineering": "R3 is the highest-ranked applicable rule: a reproducible functional defect from a Business/Enterprise org.", "escalate_to_security": "R1 applies: data from a different organization is exposed to this customer.", "page_oncall": "R2 applies: an active outage for the Enterprise organization.", "process_refund": "R4 is the highest-ranked applicable rule: route the credit/refund request to billing now.", "send_kb_article": "Only R5 applies."}, "instructions": "Apply policy SP-4 to ticket #88213 and choose the single action the support agent must take now.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "HELIO ANALYTICS — SUPPORT ROUTING POLICY SP-4 (rev. 7, effective 2026-07-01)\n\nPrecedence. When more than one rule below applies to a ticket, the agent performs ONLY the action of the highest-ranked applicable rule now. Lower-ranked requests are noted on the ticket and handled after the higher-ranked item is closed.\n\nR1  Cross-organization data exposure — the customer can see data that belongs to a different organization. Action: escalate to Security (label: escalate_to_security). Target: within 1 hour.\nR2  Active outage — no user of an Enterprise organization can log in or load dashboards. Action: page the on-call SRE (page_oncall).\nR3  Functional defect with reproducible steps, reported by a Business or Enterprise organization. Action: escalate to Product Engineering (escalate_to_engineering). SLA: first engineering response within 8 business hours.\nR4  Billing dispute, credit or refund request. Action: billing queue (process_refund). Credits are issued only once any R1–R3 item on the same ticket is resolved.\nR5  Anything else: send the matching knowledge-base article (send_kb_article).\n\nDefinitions (SP-4 §2).\n- \"Organization\" means the billing entity identified by an Org ID. An organization may own several workspaces; each workspace has its own tenant ID (tn_...). Workspaces that share an Org ID are the SAME organization for every rule in this policy, even when their tenant IDs differ.\n- \"Reproducible steps\" means the customer describes a click path or API call that produces the failure.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------\nTICKET #88213   opened 2026-09-18 16:42 CEST   channel: email\nRequester: Marta Quell (Head of RevOps)\nOrg ID: ORG-5512      Plan: Enterprise      CSM: D. Obi\nWorkspaces on this Org ID (from account profile):\n  - tn_8841  \"Main\"             created 2023-02-14\n  - tn_9120  \"EU Subsidiary\"    created 2026-03-02\nService health (status page, 2026-09-18 16:40): all systems operational. Account telemetry: 37 distinct users of ORG-5512 loaded dashboards in the last hour.\n\nMessage:\n\"Hi, our scheduled export of the 'Q3 Pipeline' report has failed twice now with error EXP-409 (Reports > Q3 Pipeline > Export > CSV — it dies at about 80%). The one time it did go through, the CSV had 212 rows tagged tenant tn_9120, which is NOT our main workspace tenant (ours is tn_8841) — so that's somebody else's data sitting in our file?! Frankly this is unacceptable for what we pay. We expect a service credit for September. Please treat this as a priority.\n— Marta\"\n"}
{"expected": "order_b", "family": "tradeoff", "group": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-03", "id": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-03", "labels": ["order_a", "order_b", "order_c", "order_d"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 494, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Order C carries a subject ID (P1) but its signed handling note says ambient 15–25 °C and do not refrigerate, so it does not require temperature control and can never consume a reefer slot. Order B's contract penalty triggers if it doesn't leave today: tomorrow's 17:00 departure + 11 h transit arrives Sunday 04:00 after the Sat 06:00 deadline, so it is P2. A and D are only P3. P2 beats P3, so B gets the slot.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "order_c", "why_hard": "The patient-specific P1 order looks decisive, but the slot rule excludes non-temperature-controlled freight, leaving a food order to outrank medicines."}, "question": {"criteria": {"order_a": "Order A (insulin replenishment) gets the slot.", "order_b": "Order B (frozen scallops under penalty contract) gets the slot.", "order_c": "Order C (biopsy sample kit) gets the slot.", "order_d": "Order D (practice vaccines) gets the slot."}, "instructions": "Which order must receive the last reefer slot on the 17:00 linehaul today under the dispatch rules?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "FROSTLINE 3PL — COLD-CHAIN DISPATCH RULES (Depot Kassel)\n\nCapacity note for Friday 2026-09-18: one (1) reefer slot remains on the 17:00 linehaul. The slot is for shipments that REQUIRE temperature control (2–8 °C or frozen). Shipments that do not require temperature control must go on ambient trucks and never consume a reefer slot.\n\nAllocation precedence for the last reefer slot (highest first):\n  P1  Patient-specific medical shipments — the manifest names a patient or carries a patient/clinical-trial subject ID.\n  P2  Shipments under a customer contract with a late-delivery penalty that will be triggered if the shipment does not leave today.\n  P3  Other medical or pharmaceutical shipments (stock replenishment, vaccines for general use, etc.).\n  P4  All other temperature-controlled freight, largest invoice value first.\nTies inside a class go to the earliest order timestamp.\n\nCandidate orders (all packed and staged at 14:30):\n\nA  Customer: Apotheken-Verbund Mitte. Content: insulin pens, 18 cartons, 2–8 °C. Manifest: \"Store replenishment — no patient reference\". Invoice EUR 61,200. Ordered 2026-09-16 09:10.\n\nB  Customer: NordSee Feinkost. Content: frozen scallops, 6 pallets, −18 °C. Contract NS-2024-07 §9: penalty EUR 4,000 if delivery to Hamburg DC is later than Sat 2026-09-19 06:00. Transit time on linehaul: 11 h. Next reefer departure after today: Sat 2026-09-19 17:00. Invoice EUR 23,900. Ordered 2026-09-17 11:45.\n\nC  Customer: Klinikum Nordhessen Studienzentrum. Content: biopsy sample return kit. Manifest line: \"Subject ID KT-0093, study ONCO-17\". Shipper's handling note (signed by lab): \"Samples fixed in formalin — ambient transport 15–25 °C; do NOT refrigerate.\" Invoice EUR 380. Ordered 2026-09-18 08:02.\n\nD  Customer: Praxis Dr. Albers. Content: influenza vaccines, 2 cartons, 2–8 °C, for general use in the practice. Invoice EUR 3,150. Ordered 2026-09-17 16:20.\n\nDispatcher's draft: \"Give the slot to C — patient samples beat everything.\"\n"}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "tradeoff", "group": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-06", "id": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-06", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 519, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "7.1: two different approvers (Aydin, Novak); lower limit is Novak's EUR 50,000 ≥ 48,500. 7.2(a): callback went to the master number on file since 2024, before the 2026-09-11 change request. 7.2(b): Jonas made the callback, Priya received the request. 7.2(c): callback Tue 09-15; Wed 16 and Thu 17 are two full business days, so Friday 09-18 is past cooling-off. 7.3: the CFO pressure is logged as a red flag but neither replaces nor blocks controls, so the payment is released.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "no", "why_hard": "The case has textbook fraud signals and a cautious clerk recommending a hold, but every explicit control is met and the manual says urgency doesn't block release."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "At least one control in 7.1 or 7.2 is not satisfied, so the payment must be held.", "true": "All controls in 7.1 and 7.2 are satisfied, so under 7.3 the payment is released."}, "instructions": "Under §7 of the control manual, must payment PF-0919-03 be released today?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "KESTREL MACHINING GmbH — ACCOUNTS PAYABLE CONTROL MANUAL, §7 Outgoing payments\n\n7.1 Dual approval. Every payment above EUR 10,000 needs two approvals from different people in the approver register, and the amount must be within the lower of the two approvers' limits.\n7.2 Bank-detail changes. When a vendor's bank details changed in the last 90 days, the payment may only be released if:\n    (a) the change was verified by a phone callback to the number stored in the vendor master record BEFORE the change request arrived (never a number taken from the change request itself), and\n    (b) the callback was made by someone other than the employee who received the change request, and\n    (c) at least 2 full business days have passed since the successful callback (cooling-off). Business days = Mon–Fri, excluding public holidays in Hesse.\n7.3 Urgency pressure from executives is a red flag that must be logged, but it neither replaces nor blocks any control; if all controls in 7.1–7.2 are met the payment is released.\n\nApprover register (excerpt): Controller M. Aydin — limit EUR 100,000; Treasury Manager P. Novak — limit EUR 50,000; CFO R. Sauer — limit EUR 250,000.\nPublic holidays in Hesse, Sep 2026: none.\n\nPAYMENT FILE PF-0919-03  (today: Friday 2026-09-18, 11:00)\nPayee: Vollmer Präzisionsteile KG     Amount: EUR 48,500.00     Invoice VP-2026-0833\nVendor master: bank details changed 2026-09-11 (request received by email by AP clerk Priya S.). Master phone number +49 69 5550 1840, on file since 2024-11-05.\nCallback log: 2026-09-15 (Tuesday) 14:20 — Jonas W. (AP) called +49 69 5550 1840, spoke to Vollmer's head of accounts, new IBAN confirmed digit by digit. Result: VERIFIED.\nApprovals: M. Aydin (Controller) 2026-09-17 16:02; P. Novak (Treasury Manager) 2026-09-18 09:40.\nEmail from R. Sauer (CFO), 2026-09-18 08:55: \"Vollmer is threatening to stop deliveries. This must go out TODAY, no excuses.\" — logged as red flag RF-221 by Priya S.\nAP clerk note: \"Classic fraud pattern: new bank details + CFO pressure + Friday. I'd hold this until Monday to be safe.\"\n"}
{"expected": "p0_fix_24h", "family": "tradeoff", "group": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-07", "id": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-07", "labels": ["out_of_scope", "p3_backlog", "p2_fix_30d", "p1_fix_7d", "p0_fix_24h"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 468, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The flag defaults OFF but has an override ON for tenant acme-demo-internal, which lives on prod-eu-1; the runbook defines production as every tenant on the prod cluster, including internal and demo tenants, and prod logs show real traffic to /v2/resize. The endpoint needs no authentication and our re-score is 9.1 ≥ 9.0, so the second P0 condition holds even with no evidence of exploitation.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "p2_fix_30d", "why_hard": "The August 'disabled everywhere' comment and the engineer's P2 call hide a later per-tenant override on a demo tenant that the runbook counts as production."}, "question": {"criteria": {"out_of_scope": "The affected asset is operated by a third party.", "p0_fix_24h": "Active exploitation in production, or CVSS ≥ 9.0 and reachable in production without authentication.", "p1_fix_7d": "CVSS ≥ 7.0, reachable in production, but P0 conditions not met.", "p2_fix_30d": "The vulnerable path is not reachable in production right now.", "p3_backlog": "Informational or hardening only."}, "instructions": "Apply the triage ladder to BB-2026-0412. Which priority must be assigned?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "ORBITAL NOTES (our SaaS) — VULNERABILITY TRIAGE LADDER, security@ runbook §3\n\nAssign the first matching priority, checking from the top:\n  P0 (fix or mitigate within 24 h): evidence of active exploitation in production, OR CVSS ≥ 9.0 and the vulnerable code path is reachable in production by unauthenticated users.\n  P1 (fix within 7 days): CVSS ≥ 7.0 and reachable in production by any user (authenticated or not).\n  P2 (fix within 30 days): the vulnerable code path is not reachable in production at present (e.g. feature flag off for every production tenant, dead code), whatever its score.\n  P3 (backlog): informational / hardening only.\n  Out of scope: the affected asset is operated by a third party, not by us.\n\"Production\" means every tenant on the prod cluster, including internal, demo and sandbox tenants hosted there. The staging cluster is not production.\n\nBUG BOUNTY REPORT BB-2026-0412 (received 2026-09-19 07:55 UTC)\nTitle: Unauthenticated RCE via crafted SVG in image resizer v2\nComponent: media-svc, endpoint GET /v2/resize?src=...\nResearcher CVSS 3.1: 9.8 — our re-score: 9.1 (Critical)\nAuth: none required; /v2/resize serves public embedded images.\nResearcher's PoC run against: staging.orbitalnotes.dev\n\nInternal checks\n- Feature flag img_resize_v2 (prod cluster config, fetched 08:30 UTC):\n    default: OFF\n    overrides: [ {tenant: \"acme-demo-internal\", value: ON, set_by: \"sales-eng\", set_at: \"2026-09-04\"} ]\n- Flag changelog comment 2026-08-02: \"img_resize_v2 disabled everywhere after perf regression.\"\n- WAF/access logs, prod, last 30 days: 1,904 requests to /v2/resize, all from tenant acme-demo-internal embeds; no payloads matching the PoC signature.\n- Tenant registry: acme-demo-internal — cluster: prod-eu-1; type: internal demo tenant (publicly reachable demo pages).\nEngineer on triage: \"Flag has been off since August, so this is a P2.\"\n"}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "tradeoff", "group": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-08", "id": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-08", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 407, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Jordan wants 5 days past Mon 09-21. §12.2 gives an automatic 48 h (2 days) because the valid DAS letter lists extended deadlines, and it does not count toward the instructor's cap and stacks with it. Dr. Reyes can add 3 days under §12.1: 2 + 3 = 5 days, landing on Sat 09-26 23:59. The total (5) is under the 7-day ceiling and it can be recorded before the original deadline, so no Dean approval is needed.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "no", "why_hard": "5 > 3 looks like it exceeds the instructor's cap; one has to notice the accommodation extension stacks and does not count toward it."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "Reaching the requested deadline needs Dean of Students approval (or is not allowed at all).", "true": "The requested new deadline is reachable using only the instructor's discretion plus the automatic accommodation extension."}, "instructions": "Can Dr. Reyes give Jordan the full extension to Sat 2026-09-26 23:59 on her own authority, without Dean of Students approval?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "HALVERSTON COLLEGE — ACADEMIC REGULATIONS 2026/27, §12 Coursework deadlines\n\n12.1 Instructor discretion. A course instructor may, on their own authority, grant a student an extension of up to 3 calendar days on a coursework deadline.\n12.2 Registered accommodations. Students with a registered accommodation letter from Disability & Access Services that lists \"extended deadlines\" receive an automatic 48-hour extension on every coursework deadline. This automatic extension does not count toward the instructor's 3-day limit in 12.1 and applies on top of any instructor extension.\n12.3 Anything beyond what 12.1 and 12.2 together allow requires written approval from the Dean of Students, and the total extension from all sources may never exceed 7 calendar days.\n12.4 Extensions must be recorded in the gradebook before the original deadline passes.\n\nEmail thread — HIST 214 (Instructor: Dr. Amaya Reyes)\nFrom: Jordan Pike, to Dr. Reyes, Tue 2026-09-15 10:02\n  \"Dear Dr. Reyes, I've had a family emergency and I don't think I can submit the Essay 2 by the deadline (Mon 2026-09-21, 23:59). Could I have 5 days, i.e. until Sat 2026-09-26 23:59? I also have an accommodation letter on file with DAS.\"\nFrom: Dr. Reyes, to Registrar helpdesk, Tue 2026-09-15 10:40\n  \"I'm happy to help Jordan, but 5 days is more than my 3-day limit — do I need to go to the Dean?\"\nRegistrar record for Jordan Pike: DAS accommodation letter #DAS-2291, valid 2026-08-01 to 2027-07-31. Listed accommodations: \"extended deadlines (48 h)\"; \"exam room with reduced distractions\".\nGradebook: no extension recorded yet for Essay 2. Today is Thu 2026-09-17.\n"}
{"expected": "refund_now", "family": "tradeoff", "group": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-12", "id": "hard-opus-b-tradeoff-12", "labels": ["wait_for_inspection", "refund_now", "offer_store_credit", "escalate_to_fraud_team", "deny_refund"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 427, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Withdrawal was notified on 08-31, 4 days after delivery on 08-27, within 14 days (RF-1). The goods arrived 2026-09-03, so the statutory refund deadline was 2026-09-17, which has already passed. RF-3 overrides the abuse-watch inspection (RF-2), so refund now and open a post-refund review. Store credit needs a customer request (RF-4), and there is no chargeback or identity mismatch for fraud referral (RF-5). The team lead's 'no exceptions' note conflicts with RF-3.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "wait_for_inspection", "why_hard": "The abuse flag and a team lead's 'no exceptions' note point to holding, but the date arithmetic triggers an explicit override."}, "question": {"criteria": {"deny_refund": "Refuse the refund.", "escalate_to_fraud_team": "Refer the case to the fraud team.", "offer_store_credit": "Offer store credit / a voucher instead of money.", "refund_now": "Pay the money refund now and open a post-refund review.", "wait_for_inspection": "Keep the refund on hold until the warehouse inspection is done."}, "instructions": "What must the agent do on case RC-77104 today under the handbook?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "BRIGHTCART (online retailer, EU) — RETURNS & REFUNDS HANDBOOK, rules for agents\n\nRF-1 Statutory withdrawal. A consumer who notifies withdrawal within 14 days of delivery is entitled to a refund. Once we have received the goods back, the refund must be paid no later than 14 calendar days after the day of receipt at our warehouse (\"statutory refund deadline\").\nRF-2 Return-abuse watch. For accounts flagged \"abuse watch\", a warehouse condition inspection is normally completed before any refund.\nRF-3 Deadline override. Inspection must never make us miss the statutory refund deadline. If the deadline falls today or has already passed and the inspection is still pending, refund immediately and open a post-refund review (the review can later lead to a deduction claim for damage).\nRF-4 Store credit may only be offered instead of money if the customer asks for it.\nRF-5 Referral to the fraud team is only for orders with a payment chargeback or identity mismatch.\n\nCASE RC-77104 (today: 2026-09-19)\nOrder 4471-KX, one espresso machine, EUR 649.00, paid by card. Delivered 2026-08-27.\nCustomer message 2026-08-31: \"I'm withdrawing from this purchase, the machine doesn't fit my kitchen. Please send return label.\"\nReturn parcel scanned as received at warehouse Duisburg: 2026-09-03 10:12.\nInspection status: PENDING (warehouse backlog, estimated completion 2026-09-24).\nAccount flags: ABUSE WATCH (4 returns in 6 months).\nPayment: no chargeback; card holder name matches account.\nCustomer message 2026-09-18: \"It's been over two weeks since you got the machine. Where is my money?\"\nTeam lead note in CRM: \"Abuse-watch accounts: no refund until inspection, no exceptions. Offer a voucher if she complains.\"\n"}
{"expected": "erase_but_retain_transaction_records", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-02", "id": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-02", "labels": ["erase_all", "restrict_all_pending_legal_hold", "erase_but_retain_transaction_records", "reject_unverified_request", "erase_marketing_only"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3069, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The request became verified on 10 Sep 2026 (authenticated privacy form, 3.1(a)). LH-2026-014 covering C-2231907 was released on 2 Sep; the active LH-2026-019 covers other IDs only (6.1). Under Amendment 2026-1 the retention is 7 years from the end of the invoice-date year (account type Consumer, so §9 does not apply). INV-17-0310448 (2017) expired end of 2024 (it escaped the Q1 clean-up only because of the then-active hold). O-21 and O-23 have no invoice, so they are not Transaction Records (2.4). But order O-18-99761 was invoiced on 3 Jan 2019 (2.9), so its Transaction Record is retained until 31 Dec 2026 and must be restricted rather than erased (4.3). Warranties ended in 2020/2021 (+6 months). Using the 28 Dec 2018 order date would expire it at end-2025 and suggest erase_all.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "erase_all", "why_hard": "The only surviving Transaction Record depends on the invoice-date definition and the amended retention period, while a same-surname active hold and a released hold invite the wrong action."}, "question": {"criteria": {"erase_all": "Erase all personal data about the Customer now; no record needs to be retained or restricted.", "erase_but_retain_transaction_records": "Erase the Customer's personal data but retain, in restricted form, one or more Transaction Records whose retention period is still running.", "erase_marketing_only": "Erase only Marketing Data; everything else is kept.", "reject_unverified_request": "Take no action beyond asking for verification, because the request is not a Verified Request.", "restrict_all_pending_legal_hold": "Erase nothing; restrict all of the Customer's data because an active Legal Hold covers the Customer ID."}, "instructions": "How must Privacy Operations handle erasure request DSR-2026-0892 for Customer ID C-2231907 under CDH-4 as amended?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "BRIGHTWATER OUTDOOR GMBH — PRIVACY OPERATIONS\nCase pack for data subject request DSR-2026-0892 (prepared for the privacy analyst on duty)\nContents: Part 1 — Customer Data Handling Standard CDH-4, version 3.1 (in force since 1 March 2024) with Amendment 2026-1; Part 2 — CRM and ERP extracts; Part 3 — correspondence; Part 4 — Legal hold register extract.\n\nPART 1 — CUSTOMER DATA HANDLING STANDARD CDH-4 (v3.1)\n\n§1 Scope\n1.1 CDH-4 governs how Brightwater stores, uses, restricts and erases personal data of customers of the web shop and the retail stores.\n1.2 Employee data, supplier contacts and job applicants are covered by separate standards (HR-7, PRC-2, TA-3).\n1.3 Where CDH-4 requires a record to be \"restricted\", the record is moved to the restricted archive: it is kept, but may only be accessed by Finance, Legal or Privacy for the purpose for which it is retained, and it is excluded from marketing, analytics and customer service tools.\n\n§2 Definitions\n2.1 \"Customer\" — a natural person with a web-shop account or a loyalty card, identified by a Customer ID in the CRM.\n2.2 \"Account Data\" — name, contact details, login credentials, saved addresses, saved payment tokens, preferences and loyalty data.\n2.3 \"Order Data\" — baskets, quotes, order confirmations, delivery tracking and order-related customer service notes.\n2.4 \"Transaction Record\" — the record of a completed sale for which an invoice was issued: the invoice itself, the corresponding payment record and the delivery confirmation. Baskets, quotes, pro-forma documents and orders cancelled before an invoice was issued are not Transaction Records, even if a payment was authorised and later released. A pro-forma document is not an invoice.\n2.5 \"Warranty Record\" — product registration and warranty claim data needed to handle a warranty claim for a product sold to the Customer.\n2.6 \"Marketing Data\" — newsletter subscription, consent log, campaign responses, segmentation attributes.\n2.7 \"Legal Hold\" — an instruction from Legal to preserve all data relating to a specified Customer ID (or IDs) because of actual or anticipated litigation, a regulatory inquiry or a payment dispute.\n2.8 \"Verified Request\" — a request that meets the verification requirements of §3.\n2.9 \"Invoice date\" — the date printed on the invoice as its date of issue. It may differ from the order date and from the delivery date.\n\n§3 Verification of requests\n3.1 A request is verified if it is (a) submitted through the privacy form while the Customer is logged into the account, or (b) sent by e-mail from the e-mail address registered on the account and confirmed by the Customer through the six-digit code sent to that address.\n3.2 A request sent from any other e-mail address or by post is verified only when the requester provides two of the following that match the CRM: an order number, the billing postcode, the loyalty card number, the last four digits of a saved card.\n3.3 A request made by a third party on behalf of a Customer requires a signed authority from the Customer in addition to verification under 3.1 or 3.2.\n3.4 Until a request is verified, no action is taken on it other than asking for verification. The response deadline in §7 runs from the date of verification.\n\n§4 Erasure requests\n4.1 On a Verified Request for erasure, Brightwater erases all personal data relating to the Customer ID, subject only to 4.2 to 4.4.\n4.2 Legal Hold. While a Legal Hold covering the Customer ID is active, nothing is erased; all data relating to that Customer ID is restricted until the hold is released, and the request is then processed under 4.1.\n4.3 Transaction Records whose retention period (§5) has not ended on the day the erasure is carried out are not erased but restricted; they are erased at the end of the retention period without a further request.\n4.4 Warranty Records are retained (restricted) while the warranty period for the product plus six months is running.\n4.5 Marketing Data is always erased immediately; the Customer's e-mail address is added to the suppression list in hashed form so that it is not re-imported from partner lists. The hashed suppression entry is not personal data held about the Customer for the purposes of 4.1.\n4.6 An erasure request is not refused because the Customer has an open balance; Finance is informed and collects on the basis of the Transaction Records.\n4.7 Loyalty points lapse on erasure. The Customer is told this in the acknowledgement.\n\n§5 Retention periods\n5.1 Transaction Records: ten (10) years from the end of the calendar year in which the invoice date falls.\n5.2 Warranty Records: the warranty period of the product plus six months.\n5.3 Customer service tickets not related to an order: three years from closure.\n5.4 CCTV images from stores: 72 hours, unless a Legal Hold applies.\n\n§6 Legal holds\n6.1 Legal Holds are entered in the Legal Hold register by Legal, identifying the Customer ID(s) covered. A hold covers only the Customer IDs listed.\n6.2 A Legal Hold remains active until Legal records its release in the register. Once released, the Customer's data is handled under the normal rules; release does not revive or cancel any pending request.\n\n§7 Response deadline\n7.1 Erasure is carried out and confirmed to the Customer within 30 days of the Verified Request.\n7.2 Erased data may persist in encrypted backups for up to 35 days until the backup cycle overwrites it. This is not a ground for refusing or delaying erasure.\n\n§8 Refusal\n8.1 An erasure request may only be refused if it is manifestly unfounded or excessive (e.g. repeated identical requests within 3 months), as decided by the Data Protection Officer.\n\n§9 Business accounts\n9.1 For business accounts (account type \"B2B\" in the CRM), contact persons' data is erased on request, but Transaction Records of the business are retained for ten (10) years from the end of the calendar year of the invoice date regardless of Amendment 2026-1, because business customers' invoices are also retained under the commercial record-keeping rules of the purchaser's contract.\n\n§10 Access and portability requests\n10.1 On a Verified Request for access, Brightwater provides a copy of all personal data relating to the Customer ID within 30 days, including restricted records, in a commonly used electronic format.\n10.2 Portability exports cover Account Data and Order Data provided by the Customer; derived segmentation attributes are excluded.\n10.3 An access request made together with an erasure request is answered first; erasure follows once the export has been downloaded or 14 days after it was made available, whichever is earlier.\n\n§11 Rectification and objection\n11.1 Inaccurate Account Data is corrected within 10 days of a Verified Request.\n11.2 An objection to marketing is handled as a request to erase Marketing Data only (4.5 applies); other data is not affected unless the Customer also requests erasure.\n11.3 Where a Customer's wording is unclear (\"stop contacting me\", \"remove me from your lists\"), Privacy asks whether erasure of the whole account is intended before acting beyond 11.2.\n\n§12 Store purchases without account\n12.1 Receipts of store purchases paid by card are Transaction Records of the store; they are linked to a Customer ID only where a loyalty card was scanned.\n12.2 Store purchases with a loyalty card scan appear in the ERP under the Customer ID with document type \"POS\".\n\nAMENDMENT 2026-1 TO CDH-4 (approved by the Data Protection Officer and the CFO, effective 1 January 2026)\nA. §5.1 is replaced by: \"Transaction Records: seven (7) years from the end of the calendar year in which the invoice date falls.\"\nB. The new §5.1 applies to all Transaction Records held on or after 1 January 2026, including records created before that date. Records whose seven-year period ended before 1 January 2026 are to be erased in the Q1 2026 clean-up run, unless restricted for another reason.\nC. §9.1 is unaffected.\nD. The Q1 2026 clean-up run was completed on 27 March 2026 for all consumer accounts without an active Legal Hold on that date.\n\nPART 2 — CRM / ERP EXTRACTS (pulled 11 September 2026)\n\nCRM search \"Kessler\" returned:\n  Customer ID C-2231907 | Miriam Kessler | account type: Consumer | registered e-mail m.kessler.home@mailbox.example | billing postcode 79098 | loyalty card LC-0081-4410 | account created 14 February 2016 | newsletter: subscribed (double opt-in 2016)\n  Customer ID C-2213907 | Martin Kessler | account type: Consumer | registered e-mail kessler.martin@netpost.example | billing postcode 79104 | account created 2 June 2021\n  Customer ID B-0045120 | Kessler Holzbau KG | account type: B2B | contact person: Martin Kessler\n\nERP documents for Customer ID C-2231907:\n  O-17-55102 | order 9 March 2017 | invoice INV-17-0310448 dated 14 March 2017 | delivered 16 March 2017 | trekking tent \"Alpspitz 2\" | paid by card\n  O-18-99761 | order 28 December 2018 (web shop, holiday sale) | invoice INV-19-0000412 dated 3 January 2019 (invoicing batch suspended 24 Dec 2018 – 2 Jan 2019) | delivered 7 January 2019 | down jacket \"Feldberg\" | paid by PayPal\n  O-21-30456 | order 11 May 2021 | cancelled by customer 11 May 2021 before dispatch; no invoice | basket only\n  O-23-71220 | order 2 October 2023 | pro-forma PF-23-71220 issued 2 October 2023 (bank-transfer prepayment); payment received 5 October 2023; order cancelled by Brightwater 9 October 2023 (item discontinued) before dispatch; refund of the prepayment 10 October 2023; no invoice issued\nWarranty registrations (C-2231907):\n  \"Alpspitz 2\" tent, registered 20 March 2017, manufacturer warranty 3 years (to 16 March 2020)\n  \"Feldberg\" jacket, registered 9 January 2019, Brightwater warranty 2 years (to 7 January 2021)\nCustomer service tickets (C-2231907): CS-23-18820 (October 2023, \"where is my refund?\"), closed 12 October 2023; CS-26-04471 (August 2026, payment dispute), closed 3 September 2026.\nLoyalty balance: 1,240 points.\nOpen balance: none.\n\nERP note on INV-17-0310448: included in the Q1 2026 clean-up run? — \"No: Customer ID C-2231907 excluded from the Q1 2026 run (Legal Hold LH-2026-014 active on 27 March 2026).\"\n\n3 September 2026, customer service call log CS-26-04471 (agent J. Brandl): \"Caller Miriam Kessler asked whether the chargeback matter is finished. Informed her that our Legal team closed it. Caller said she will close her account. No other request recorded.\"\nPOS documents for Customer ID C-2231907: none (loyalty card never scanned in store since 2019 system migration; pre-2019 POS data was not migrated).\n\nPART 3 — CORRESPONDENCE\n\n28 August 2026, e-mail to privacy@brightwater-outdoor.example from miriam.k.studio@fastmail.example:\n  \"Hello, I no longer want to be your customer. Please delete everything you have about me, including my order history. Miriam Kessler.\"\n29 August 2026, Privacy reply to miriam.k.studio@fastmail.example: \"We need to verify your identity before we can act. Please either log into your account and use the privacy form, or reply with two of: an order number, your billing postcode, your loyalty card number, the last four digits of a saved card.\"\n10 September 2026, privacy form submission, session authenticated as Customer ID C-2231907 (login 10 September 2026 19:42): \"Request type: erasure of all my data. Comment: see my e-mail of 28 August.\"\n10 September 2026, automatic acknowledgement sent to m.kessler.home@mailbox.example.\n\nPART 4 — LEGAL HOLD REGISTER (extract, entries touching the name \"Kessler\")\n\nLH-2026-014 | placed 12 February 2026 | Customer ID(s): C-2231907 | reason: payment dispute on order O-23-71220 (customer's bank raised a chargeback in January 2026 claiming the October 2023 refund was never received) | status: RELEASED 2 September 2026 by Legal (dispute closed in Brightwater's favour; bank withdrew chargeback) | note: preserve CS tickets and payment records until release\nLH-2026-019 | placed 6 July 2026 | Customer ID(s): C-2213907, B-0045120 | reason: product liability claim (roof-rack failure) | status: ACTIVE\nLH-2024-006 | placed 3 April 2024 | Customer ID(s): C-2231907 | reason: data export request audit | status: RELEASED 30 April 2024\n\nAnalyst checklist (standard form, to be completed): verification ☐  legal hold ☐  transaction records ☐  warranty records ☐  marketing ☐  loyalty ☐\n"}
{"expected": "eur_400", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-03", "id": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-03", "labels": ["eur_0", "eur_150", "eur_200", "eur_300", "eur_400", "eur_600"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3042, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Cause: a hydraulic leak found at pre-flight inspection with no manufacturer/authority notice is not an Extraordinary Circumstance under Revision 3 (F6; the flight was operated in August 2026, so the Revision 2 F5 in the template is superseded). Arrival Time is the door opening (B3): 21:14Z = 00:14 Kaarmo time vs scheduled 21:10 local (18:10Z) -> 3 h 04 min; on-block (2 h 59) is not decisive. Distance is the great-circle PVL-KRM = 3,440 km (B5; not 1,960 + 1,610 = 3,570, and KRM not KVJ) -> EUR 400 band. She was offered Rerouting, but the E2 reduction only applies if the delay does not exceed 3 hours; 3 h 04 exceeds it, so no reduction: EUR 400. Adding leg distances gives EUR 300 (or 150 with reduction); on-block or the old F5 gives 0.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "eur_0", "why_hard": "The door-opening definition, the great-circle distance rule, the Revision 3 change to technical defects and the rerouting reduction threshold all interact; a same-surname passenger on another PNR and a pre-filled template point elsewhere."}, "question": {"criteria": {"eur_0": "No compensation is payable.", "eur_150": "Compensation of EUR 150 is payable.", "eur_200": "Compensation of EUR 200 is payable.", "eur_300": "Compensation of EUR 300 is payable.", "eur_400": "Compensation of EUR 400 is payable.", "eur_600": "Compensation of EUR 600 is payable."}, "instructions": "What fixed compensation does Aurelle Air owe Elena Saarinen (PNR 7QK4ZD) under the PSC?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "AURELLE AIR — CUSTOMER RELATIONS, COMPENSATION DESK\nClaim file CR-26-08-44187. Documents: (1) Passenger Service Commitment (PSC), edition 2025, as amended by Revision 3; (2) superseded PSC Revision 2 list of extraordinary circumstances (retained in the claim template); (3) Annex D distance table extract; (4) booking and operational records; (5) claim correspondence.\n\n(1) PASSENGER SERVICE COMMITMENT — EDITION 2025 (in force for flights from 1 April 2025), WITH REVISION 3 (in force for flights operated from 1 February 2026)\n\nPart A — General\nA1 The PSC is part of Aurelle Air's Conditions of Carriage. It gives passengers rights to care, rerouting, refunds and fixed compensation in the cases described below. It does not limit any rights passengers have under the law of the country of departure.\nA2 The PSC applies to passengers holding a confirmed reservation on a flight operated by Aurelle Air, who presented themselves for check-in by the published deadline (or, with online check-in, at the gate by the boarding deadline).\nA3 Codeshare flights marketed by Aurelle Air but operated by another airline are handled under the operating airline's conditions.\n\nPart B — Definitions\nB1 \"Booking\" — one or more flights under one booking reference (PNR), ticketed together. Flights booked under separate PNRs are separate Bookings even when the passengers travel together.\nB2 \"Final Destination\" — the destination on the ticket of the last flight of the Booking. Alternative connecting flights taken because of a disruption do not change the Final Destination.\nB3 \"Arrival Time\" — the moment at which at least one door of the aircraft is opened after the aircraft has reached its parking position, passengers being permitted to leave the aircraft. Touchdown time and on-block time are recorded for operational purposes and are not the Arrival Time.\nB4 \"Arrival Delay\" — the difference between the scheduled arrival time at the Final Destination of the Booking and the Arrival Time at the Final Destination, both expressed in the same time reference.\nB5 \"Distance\" — the great-circle distance between the airport of first departure and the Final Destination of the Booking, as listed in Annex D. For Bookings with connections, the distances of the individual flights are not added.\nB6 \"Extraordinary Circumstances\" — the circumstances listed in Part F, which could not have been avoided even if all reasonable measures had been taken.\nB7 \"Rerouting\" — carriage to the Final Destination under comparable conditions at the earliest opportunity, offered by Aurelle Air after a cancellation, denied boarding or missed connection.\n\nPart C — Care\nC1 Meals and refreshments in reasonable relation to the waiting time, from 2 hours of expected delay (short flights), 3 hours (medium), 4 hours (long).\nC2 Hotel accommodation where an overnight stay becomes necessary.\nC3 Care is provided in kind (vouchers) or reimbursed against receipts within reasonable limits.\nC4 Passengers are offered two telephone calls or e-mails free of charge.\nC5 Care is not refused because the cause of the delay is an Extraordinary Circumstance; E3 limits fixed compensation only.\n\nPart D — Refunds and rerouting\nD1 Where a flight is cancelled, or a delay of 5 hours or more is expected, passengers may choose a refund of the unused part of the ticket instead of travel.\nD2 A passenger who misses a connection within the same Booking because of a delay of an earlier flight is offered Rerouting.\n\nPart E — Fixed compensation\nE1 A passenger whose Arrival Delay at the Final Destination is 3 hours or more receives fixed compensation according to the Distance:\n  Distance up to 1,500 km: EUR 250\n  Distance more than 1,500 km and up to 3,500 km: EUR 400\n  Distance more than 3,500 km: EUR 300 if the Arrival Delay is 3 hours or more but less than 4 hours; EUR 600 if the Arrival Delay is 4 hours or more.\nE2 Compensation under E1 is reduced by 50 % where the passenger was offered Rerouting and the Arrival Delay at the Final Destination does not exceed: 2 hours (Distance up to 1,500 km), 3 hours (more than 1,500 up to 3,500 km), 4 hours (more than 3,500 km).\nE3 No compensation is payable where the delay or cancellation was caused by Extraordinary Circumstances, or where the passenger was informed of a cancellation at least 14 days before scheduled departure.\nE4 Compensation is paid per passenger, in cash, bank transfer or (with the passenger's written agreement) travel vouchers. Infants travelling free of charge on a lap are not entitled to compensation.\nE5 Where a delay on an earlier flight of the Booking causes a missed connection, the Arrival Delay at the Final Destination is decisive; the cause of the delay of the earlier flight is examined under E3.\nE6 Claims must be made within 2 years of the scheduled date of the flight.\n\nPart F — Extraordinary Circumstances (as amended by Revision 3)\nF1 Meteorological conditions incompatible with the safe operation of the flight.\nF2 Air traffic management decisions (slot restrictions, airspace closures) and strikes by staff not employed by Aurelle Air or its contractors.\nF3 Security risks, political instability, and airport closures ordered by authorities.\nF4 Bird strikes and damage to the aircraft caused by third parties (e.g. ground-handling vehicles of other operators).\nF5 Hidden manufacturing defects that affect the safety of the flight and that the aircraft manufacturer or an authority has notified to operators.\nF6 Revision 3 note: technical problems that arise in connection with the maintenance or operation of the aircraft, including those discovered during routine checks or pre-flight inspections, are not Extraordinary Circumstances unless covered by F5. Staff shortages and strikes by Aurelle Air's own staff are not Extraordinary Circumstances.\n\nPart G — Downgrading\nG1 Passengers placed in a lower class than booked receive a refund of 30 % (up to 1,500 km), 50 % (1,500–3,500 km) or 75 % (above 3,500 km) of the ticket price of the affected flight.\n\nPart H — Baggage\nH1 Delayed checked baggage: reimbursement of reasonable essential purchases up to EUR 50 per day for up to 5 days, against receipts.\nH2 Lost baggage: liability as set out in the Conditions of Carriage, Article 17; claims within 21 days of the date the baggage should have been delivered.\nH3 Baggage delays do not give rise to fixed compensation under Part E.\n\nPart I — Claims handling\nI1 Claims are assessed per passenger. A claim form may cover several passengers of one Booking; passengers on other Bookings must claim separately.\nI2 The desk establishes the facts from the operational log (times in UTC) and converts them to the local time of the relevant airport using the time references in Annex D.\nI3 The automatic pre-fill in the claim template is an aid for the desk and is not binding.\nI4 Payment is made within 30 days of acceptance of the claim.\nI5 Travel agencies may submit claims on behalf of passengers with a signed mandate; payment is then made to the passenger's account unless the mandate says otherwise.\n\nPart J — Passengers with reduced mobility and unaccompanied minors\nJ1 Passengers with reduced mobility and their companions, and unaccompanied minors, receive care under Part C as soon as possible irrespective of the delay thresholds.\nJ2 Compensation under Part E is not increased for these passengers.\n\nPart K — Goodwill gestures\nK1 Customer Relations may offer vouchers as a goodwill gesture where no compensation is due. Goodwill vouchers are not compensation under Part E and are not offset against it.\nK2 Where compensation under Part E is paid, meal vouchers and hotel costs under Part C are not deducted.\nK3 Where a passenger accepts a voucher under E4 instead of cash, the voucher value is 130 % of the cash amount.\n\n(2) SUPERSEDED — PSC REVISION 2, PART F (flights operated 1 April 2025 – 31 January 2026), as printed in the claim template\n\"F1 Weather. F2 ATC decisions and strikes. F3 Security and airport closures. F4 Bird strikes. F5 Unexpected technical defects, including defects discovered during pre-flight inspection, where the aircraft had been maintained in accordance with the approved maintenance programme.\"\n\n(3) ANNEX D — DISTANCE TABLE (EXTRACT), great-circle, km\nPortavela (PVL) – Dornstadt (DRN) ........ 1,960\nDornstadt (DRN) – Kaarmo (KRM) ........... 1,610\nPortavela (PVL) – Kaarmo (KRM) ........... 3,440\nPortavela (PVL) – Kaarmo Vesijärvi (KVJ) . 3,515   (secondary airport of Kaarmo)\nPortavela (PVL) – Lindholm (LDH) ......... 2,280\nLindholm (LDH) – Kaarmo (KRM) ............ 1,390\nTime references: Portavela local = UTC+1; Dornstadt local = UTC+2; Kaarmo local = UTC+3 (all summer time in August 2026).\n\n(4) BOOKING AND OPERATIONAL RECORDS\nBooking PNR 7QK4ZD, ticketed 2 June 2026, passenger SAARINEN/ELENA MS, adult:\n  AU 311  PVL 11:45 → DRN 16:05 (local times), 14 August 2026\n  AU 874  DRN 17:05 → KRM 21:10 (local times), 14 August 2026 (scheduled connection 60 minutes)\nBooking PNR 7QL0BA, ticketed 3 June 2026, passenger SAARINEN/JUHANI MR, adult, same flights and dates (booked separately by the same travel agency).\nCheck-in: both passengers checked in online on 13 August and were at the PVL gate before the boarding deadline.\n\nFlight AU 311, 14 August 2026, aircraft registration EC-AUR:\n  Pre-flight inspection by Aurelle Air engineers at PVL found a hydraulic fluid leak at the left main landing-gear actuator; a seal was replaced. No manufacturer or authority notice applied to the part. Departure 12:57 local (72 minutes late); arrival DRN 17:14 local (69 minutes late).\n  Ground handling at PVL was provided by Portavela Ground Services, whose staff were not on strike. The PVL tower reported no slot restrictions for AU 311.\nTechnical log extract, EC-AUR, 14 August 2026, PVL: \"Pre-flight walk-around 10:52L: fluid trace L MLG actuator. Seal P/N 42-7719-03 replaced from station stock. Leak check satisfactory 12:31L. Last A-check 2 August 2026, no findings. No airworthiness directive or service bulletin applicable to P/N 42-7719-03.\" Crew report AU 878 (captain): \"Door 1L opened after jet-bridge docking; slight delay of jet-bridge operator.\" KRM station report: \"AU 878 on stand 14; jet-bridge docked 21:13Z.\"\nMissed connection: AU 874 departed DRN 17:08 local without the two passengers.\nRerouting: at the DRN transfer desk both passengers were offered AU 878 DRN 19:55 → KRM 23:55 local. Elena Saarinen accepted AU 878 (rebooked under PNR 7QK4ZD). Juhani Saarinen declined and instead accepted an offer on AU 1402 DRN 18:20 → LDH 20:05 and a connecting flight of a partner airline LDH 21:10 → KRM 23:50 local (the latter rebooked on a new PNR 7QM9XX by the transfer desk).\nFlight AU 878, 14 August 2026, operational log (UTC): off-block 17:02Z; take-off 17:14Z; touchdown KRM 21:06Z; on-block 21:09Z; forward left door opened, passengers released 21:14Z.\nPartner flight LDH–KRM, 14 August 2026: arrived at the gate KRM 23:40 local; door opened 23:43 local.\nCare: meal vouchers EUR 15 each issued at DRN.\n\nEarlier claims on file for the name SAARINEN/ELENA:\n  CR-25-11-20931 — AU 522 KRM → PVL, 2 November 2025, Arrival Delay 3 h 41 min, cause: ATC slot restriction at KRM (Revision 2 F2); rejected under E3; not appealed.\n  CR-24-06-10077 — SAARINEN/ELENA MRS, frequent-flyer no. AU 4410-2281 (different date of birth), AU 305 PVL → DRN, 6 June 2024, Arrival Delay 3 h 22 min, cause: crew shortage; paid EUR 400.\nFare paid, PNR 7QK4ZD: EUR 486.40 (economy class, both flights). No downgrade occurred.\n\n(5) CLAIM CORRESPONDENCE\n18 August 2026, online claim form, PNR 7QK4ZD, claimant Elena Saarinen: \"Our first flight was late because of a technical problem, I missed my connection and arrived in Kaarmo after midnight. Please pay the compensation due under your PSC.\" Bank details provided.\n18 August 2026, online claim form, PNR 7QL0BA, claimant Juhani Saarinen: \"Same as my wife's claim.\"\nDesk template pre-fill (automatic, based on the operational database): \"AU 878 on-block 21:09Z; scheduled arrival AU 874 21:10 local; delay category: under 3 h. Cause code of AU 311: TECH-HYD. Template check box 'Extraordinary circumstances (PSC Part F5)': suggested YES.\"\n"}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-04", "id": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-04", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3010, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Publication is the portal release on Fri 11 Dec 2026 (1.5), after 1 Nov 2026, so the amended 15-working-day limit applies (S/2026/41 R1, R3). Counting from the next working day and excluding the public holidays and the closure days 24 Dec-1 Jan (1.4): 14-18 Dec (5), 21-23 Dec (8), 4-8 Jan (13), 11 Jan (14), 12 Jan (15). Lodged Tue 12 Jan 22:47, before 23:59: in time. Ground (a) (20 minutes lost in the ES3301 exam, not added back) is within 4.1; the marking complaints are academic judgement and are struck out (4.3), not fatal. Stage 1 is not required for a classification appeal (5.2), and no graduation ceremony was attended (2.3). The old 10-day limit or counting closure days as working days (15th day = 6 Jan) would make it late.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "no", "why_hard": "The deadline lands exactly on the lodging day only when the amendment's commencement rule, the portal-release definition and the closure-day definition are all applied."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "The appeal is inadmissible (out of time, no admissible ground, or another bar in Sections 2-5).", "true": "The appeal meets the time limit and at least one ground is admissible, and no other admissibility bar applies."}, "instructions": "Is appeal APL-2027-0038 admissible at screening (at least in part) under the AAR as amended?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "HARWOOD METROPOLITAN UNIVERSITY — ACADEMIC REGISTRY, APPEALS OFFICE\nAdmissibility screening: appeal APL-2027-0038\nDocuments in this file: (1) Academic Appeals Regulations (AAR), 2025/26 edition, as amended by Senate Resolution S/2026/41; (2) Academic Calendar 2026/27 extract; (3) Student Complaints Procedure extract (for reference in cases transferred between procedures); (4) student record extracts; (5) the appeal submission and related correspondence.\n\n(1) ACADEMIC APPEALS REGULATIONS, 2025/26 EDITION\n\nSection 1 — Interpretation\n1.1 These Regulations apply to appeals against decisions of Boards of Examiners and of Progression Boards on taught programmes.\n1.2 \"Student\" includes a person whose registration ended within the previous six months.\n1.3 \"Result\" means a decision of a Board of Examiners or Progression Board recorded on the student record: a module mark, a progression decision, or an award and classification decision.\n1.4 \"Working day\" means Monday to Friday, excluding public holidays in England and the University closure days listed in the Academic Calendar. Days on which teaching does not take place (reading weeks, vacations) are working days unless they are closure days.\n1.5 \"Publication\" of a Result means its release to the student on the Student Portal. E-mail notifications that a Result is available are courtesy messages and do not affect the date of Publication.\n1.6 Periods expressed in working days run from the working day following the event that starts them. A period ends at 23:59 UK time on its last working day.\n1.7 \"Lodged\" means received through the Appeals form (AP1) on the Student Portal. Where the Student Portal is unavailable for more than four hours on the last day of a period, an appeal e-mailed to appeals@harwood-met.example on that day is treated as lodged.\n\nSection 2 — Who may appeal\n2.1 Any Student may appeal against a Result that affects them.\n2.2 An appeal may not be made by a third party except with the student's written consent.\n2.3 A student who has attended a graduation ceremony and accepted the award to which the appeal relates may not appeal against the classification of that award.\n\nSection 3 — Time limits\n3.1 An appeal must be lodged within 10 working days of Publication of the Result.\n3.2 An appeal lodged out of time is not admissible unless the student shows, with independent evidence, that they were prevented from lodging it in time by circumstances beyond their control; in that case it must be lodged within 5 working days after those circumstances ended.\n3.3 Time limits are not extended because a student is awaiting the outcome of an informal discussion, a request for feedback or a request for a re-count of marks, unless the Appeals Office agreed an extension in writing before the time limit expired.\n\nSection 4 — Grounds\n4.1 An appeal may be made only on one or more of the following grounds:\n  (a) a material procedural irregularity in the conduct of the assessment or of the Board's decision, which may have affected the Result;\n  (b) circumstances affecting the student's performance which, for good reason, the student was unable to disclose before the Board met;\n  (c) evidence of prejudice or bias, or of a reasonable perception of bias, on the part of an examiner.\n4.2 Disagreement with the academic judgement of examiners is not a ground of appeal. Academic judgement includes the marks awarded, the assessment of the quality of work and the application of marking criteria.\n4.3 An appeal which includes matters outside 4.1 is admissible in respect of those grounds that fall within 4.1; the other matters are struck out at screening.\n4.4 An appeal consisting only of matters outside 4.1 is not admissible.\n\nSection 5 — Stage 1 (informal resolution)\n5.1 Before lodging an appeal, a student must first raise the matter with the Programme Leader (Stage 1) and allow five working days for a response. The Stage 1 outcome or the expiry of five working days without a response must be recorded on the AP1 form.\n5.2 Stage 1 does not apply where the appeal is against an award and classification decision of a Board of Examiners, or where the matter concerns the conduct of the Programme Leader.\n5.3 Raising a matter under Stage 1 does not stop the time limit in Section 3 running.\n\nSection 6 — Evidence\n6.1 Documentary evidence should accompany the AP1 form. Evidence not available when the appeal is lodged may be submitted within 10 working days after lodging.\n6.2 Medical evidence must be from a registered practitioner and must cover the relevant dates.\n\nSection 7 — Screening and outcomes\n7.1 The Appeals Office screens each appeal for admissibility (Sections 2–5) within 10 working days of lodging.\n7.2 Admissible appeals are referred to an Appeals Panel. Inadmissible appeals receive a Completion of Procedures letter.\n\nSection 8 — Appeals Panel\n8.1 An Appeals Panel consists of a chair from the Senate, an academic from another faculty and, where the student requests it, a student member nominated by the Students' Union.\n8.2 The Panel considers the written submission, the evidence and the response of the Board of Examiners. The student may attend and be accompanied by a friend or a Students' Union adviser.\n8.3 The Panel may (a) dismiss the appeal; (b) refer the matter back to the Board of Examiners for reconsideration, with directions; (c) where the ground in 4.1(a) is upheld, direct that the student be offered a further attempt at the affected assessment without penalty.\n8.4 The Panel does not substitute its own academic judgement for that of the examiners.\n\nSection 9 — Completion of procedures\n9.1 A Completion of Procedures letter is issued at the end of the internal process. The student may then take the matter to the independent external reviewer within 12 months.\n9.2 A student whose appeal is found inadmissible at screening may ask the Pro-Vice-Chancellor (Education) to review the screening decision within 5 working days of the decision.\n\nSection 10 — Records and confidentiality\n10.1 Appeal files are retained for six years after the Completion of Procedures letter.\n10.2 Information provided in an appeal is shared only with those who need it to consider the appeal.\n\nSection 11 — Appeals against progression decisions (Progression Boards)\n11.1 A student required to resit or repeat may appeal under these Regulations. Stage 1 applies to all progression appeals.\n11.2 A progression appeal does not suspend the resit; the student should sit the resit while the appeal is pending.\n11.3 For progression decisions made in the summer cycle, the time limit in 3.1 is 10 working days irrespective of any amendment, because resit arrangements begin in August.\n\nSENATE RESOLUTION S/2026/41 (approved 8 July 2026)\nR1 In AAR 3.1, \"10 working days\" is replaced by \"15 working days\".\nR2 In AAR 1.7, \"four hours\" is replaced by \"two hours\".\nR3 Commencement: R1 applies to Results published on or after 1 November 2026. R2 applies from 1 September 2026.\nR4 No other change is made to the AAR. The 2026/27 edition of the AAR will be published consolidating these changes.\n\n(2) ACADEMIC CALENDAR 2026/27 — EXTRACT\nAutumn term: 21 September – 11 December 2026. Spring term: 11 January – 26 March 2027.\nPublic holidays in England in the period: Friday 25 December 2026; Monday 28 December 2026 (Boxing Day, substitute day); Friday 1 January 2027; Friday 26 March 2027; Monday 29 March 2027.\nUniversity closure days: Thursday 24 December 2026 to Friday 1 January 2027 inclusive. During closure the Student Portal remains available but the Appeals Office is not staffed.\nExamination periods: 4–15 January 2027 (autumn resits and January examinations).\nBoards of Examiners (Autumn Completion): 10 December 2026. Graduation ceremonies (winter): 20 January 2027.\n\n(3) STUDENT COMPLAINTS PROCEDURE — EXTRACT (for reference)\nSCP 2.1 Complaints about services (accommodation, library, IT) are not appeals and are handled under this procedure; the time limit is 30 calendar days from the event complained of.\nSCP 2.4 Where a submission raises both a complaint and an appeal, the Appeals Office screens the appeal part under the AAR and transfers the complaint part.\n\n(4) STUDENT RECORD EXTRACTS\nSearch \"Adebayo-Clarke\" returned two records:\n  Student 21045582 | Tolu Adebayo-Clarke | BSc (Hons) Environmental Science, final year, autumn completion (deferred final assessments from summer 2026) | Board of Examiners (Autumn Completion) 10 December 2026: award BSc (Hons) Environmental Science, classification Lower Second (2:2) | Result released on Student Portal: Friday 11 December 2026, 16:00 | notification e-mail \"Your results are available\": Monday 14 December 2026, 09:05 | graduation ceremony booking: none.\n  Student 23117094 | Toluwani Adebayo-Clarke | BA (Hons) Business Management, year 2 | Progression Board 3 December 2026: resit required in module BM2204 | Result released: 4 December 2026.\nAssessment record, student 21045582, module ES3301 \"Environmental Impact Assessment\" (final examination, 23 November 2026, 09:30, Sports Hall B): invigilation report IR-26-1123-SHB records \"Fire alarm 10:12–10:32, candidates evacuated; examination resumed 10:36 and ended at the originally scheduled finishing time 12:30. Lost time not added (chief invigilator instructed by the Examinations Office duty phone).\" Scheduled duration 3 hours.\nES3301 mark: 52. Classification borderline zone per programme regulations: 58–59.9 (not reached; overall weighted average 57.4).\n\nExaminations Office log for ES3301, 23 November 2026: \"Duty phone 10:40: Sports Hall B chief invigilator asked whether to extend. Advised to finish at 12:30 because the hall was booked for 13:00 (error — Examinations Regulation ER 7.4 requires lost time to be added). Incident referred to Head of Examinations 24 November.\"\nProgramme regulations, BSc Environmental Science: classification is based on the weighted average of level-5 (30 %) and level-6 (70 %) modules; ES3301 carries 20 of 120 level-6 credits.\nStudent 21045582 — other records: no disciplinary proceedings; no extenuating circumstances claim submitted in 2026; tuition fees paid.\n\n(5) SUBMISSION AND CORRESPONDENCE\n15 December 2026, e-mail from student 21045582 to the Programme Leader (Dr P. Nakamura): \"I think my ES3301 exam mark is too low and I'd like to know how the fire alarm was handled.\" Reply 17 December 2026: \"Marks cannot be changed informally. You may use the appeals procedure.\"\n21 December 2026, e-mail from the student to the Examinations Office asking for feedback on the ES3301 script. Reply 22 December: feedback will be available from 11 January 2027.\n4 January 2027, e-mail from the student to appeals@harwood-met.example: \"I am still waiting for feedback on my ES3301 script. Can the appeal deadline be extended until I have it?\" Reply from the Appeals Office, 5 January 2027: \"We cannot extend time limits to wait for feedback. You may lodge your appeal now and send evidence later under AAR 6.1.\"\n8 January 2027, the student booked an appointment with a Students' Union adviser for 12 January 2027, 14:00 (attended).\nAP1 form lodged on the Student Portal: Tuesday 12 January 2027, 22:47 UK time.\n  Result appealed: award and classification decision of 10 December 2026.\n  Grounds ticked: (a) procedural irregularity — \"In ES3301 we lost 20 minutes because of the fire alarm and the time was not given back; with the full time I would have finished question 4.\" Other matters: \"In addition the second marker was far too harsh in ES3204 and my dissertation deserved a first.\"\n  Stage 1: \"Discussed with Programme Leader 15–17 December 2026 (not required for classification appeals, but done).\"\n  Evidence: \"Invigilation report IR-26-1123-SHB (requested from Examinations Office); will follow.\"\n  Declaration: the student has not attended a graduation ceremony.\nPortal status log, 12 January 2027: available all day; no outage recorded.\n13 January 2027, Examinations Office to Appeals Office: copy of invigilation report IR-26-1123-SHB forwarded for file APL-2027-0038.\n"}
{"expected": 1, "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-05", "id": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-05", "labels": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3046, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Licences held on 31 Aug 2026: 100 (OF-1) + 15 (OF-2) = 115; OF-3 starts 1 Sep (5.2) and the unsigned 20-licence draft never counted. Named Users: 96 prod accounts = 94 individuals (two hold -admin accounts); parental leave since 15 Jun is only 78 days (< 90, B1.1) so counts; 3 accounts disabled 20 Aug (< 30 days before) are active (1.7), the 4 disabled 15 Jul are not; kiosk = 6 Named Users (1.5); 8 Pellam contractors count (no Tessellate confirmation, 3.2); svc-render-2 interactive use makes a.lindqvist a Named User (+1, 1.6); kestrel-qa was not registered on the Measurement Date (receipt confirmed 5 Sep), so its 5 testers count (the 4 engineers already counted, B2.3); kestrel-train accounts are registered and duplicates anyway; viewer links are excluded. Total 94+3+6+8+1+5 = 117 -> shortfall 2 -> level 1. Counting OF-3 or excluding QA gives compliant; counting all 9 QA accounts or all 7 disabled gives 6 short (level 2).", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "0", "why_hard": "Eight inventory lines each hinge on a different definition or amendment (active account, shared/service accounts, contractor rule, registration timing, per-individual dedup, licence start date), and the total lands just above entitlement."}, "question": {"criteria": ["Compliant: Named Users do not exceed Licences held on the Measurement Date", "Shortfall of 1 to 5 Licences", "Shortfall of 6 to 15 Licences", "Shortfall of more than 15 Licences"], "instructions": "Under the ELA as amended, what is Kestrel's licence position for the 31 August 2026 Measurement Date? Output the level index.", "type": "score"}, "split": "public", "state": "KESTREL MARINE SYSTEMS AS — IT ASSET MANAGEMENT\nLicence true-up worksheet: Tessellate CAD Enterprise, measurement date 31 August 2026\nPrepared for the licence compliance review before submission of the annual True-Up Report to Tessellate Software Ltd (due 30 September 2026).\n\n======================================================================\nA. ENTERPRISE LICENCE AGREEMENT TES-ELA-2023-118 (signed 1 March 2023) — EXTRACTS\n======================================================================\n1. Definitions\n1.1 \"Software\" — Tessellate CAD Enterprise, including the desktop client, the web viewer and the rendering server components.\n1.2 \"Licence\" — a named-user subscription licence to use the Software. One Licence permits one Named User to use the Software on any number of devices.\n1.3 \"Licensee Affiliates\" — entities controlling, controlled by or under common control with the Licensee; their use counts as the Licensee's use.\n1.4 \"Named User\" — an individual human being who is authorised by the Licensee to access the Software and has an active account on the Measurement Date. An individual is one Named User regardless of how many accounts the individual holds.\n1.5 Shared accounts. Where an account is used by more than one individual, each individual who used that account during the three months ending on the Measurement Date is a Named User. The Licensee shall keep login records sufficient to identify such individuals.\n1.6 Service accounts. An account used solely by software processes through the rendering-server API, without interactive login, is a \"Service Account\" and is not a Named User. If an individual logs in interactively through a Service Account at any time during the three months ending on the Measurement Date, that individual is a Named User.\n1.7 \"Active account\" — any account that exists on the Measurement Date, unless it was disabled or deleted at least 30 days before the Measurement Date.\n1.8 \"Measurement Date\" — 31 August of each year of the term.\n1.9 \"Production Environment\" — any installation of the Software other than a registered Non-Production Environment (see Amendment No. 2).\n\n2. Grant of licence and restrictions\n2.1 Tessellate grants the Licensee a non-exclusive, non-transferable right to use the Software during the term for its internal business purposes, up to the number of Licences held.\n2.2 The Licensee shall not (a) sublicense, rent or lease the Software; (b) use the Software to provide design services to third parties other than its own customers under contracts for vessels designed or built by the Licensee; (c) circumvent licence controls.\n2.3 The Software may be installed in any country in which the Licensee or its Licensee Affiliates operate, subject to export control laws.\n2.4 Plug-ins developed by the Licensee using the Tessellate SDK may be used without additional licences, but each individual using them requires a Licence as a Named User.\n\n3. Use by third parties\n3.1 The Licensee may permit its contractors and outsourcing providers to use the Software solely for the Licensee's internal business purposes.\n3.2 Contractors and outsourcing providers who use the Software under 3.1 are Named Users of the Licensee and require Licences, unless the contractor uses the contractor's own Tessellate licences under the contractor's own agreement and the Licensee has received written confirmation of this from Tessellate.\n3.3 The Licensee is responsible for the compliance of such third parties.\n\n4. Fees\n4.1 Subscription fees are invoiced annually in advance on 1 March for all Licences held.\n4.2 Licences added during a subscription year are invoiced pro rata from their licence start date to the next 1 March.\n4.3 List price for 2026: EUR 2,340 per Licence per year.\n\n5. True-up\n5.1 Each year the Licensee reports the number of Named Users as of the Measurement Date.\n5.2 The number of Licences held on the Measurement Date is the number of Licences under all Order Forms whose licence start date is on or before the Measurement Date.\n5.3 If the number of Named Users exceeds the number of Licences held, the Licensee purchases the shortfall at the then-current list price, with back-dated subscription fees for 12 months.\n5.4 A true-up shortfall of more than 15 Licences, or of more than 10 % of Licences held, entitles Tessellate to conduct an audit at the Licensee's expense.\n\n6. Audit\n6.1 Tessellate may audit the Licensee's use of the Software once per year on 30 days' notice, in addition to any audit under 5.4.\n6.2 The Licensee shall provide login records, identity management exports and Order Forms on request.\n6.3 If an audit reveals a shortfall that the Licensee did not report in its True-Up Report, the Licensee pays the shortfall at 125 % of list price.\n\n7. Term\n7.1 The ELA runs until 28 February 2028 and may be renewed for further five-year terms.\n\n8. Support and maintenance\n8.1 Support is included in the subscription fee. Support cases may be raised by up to five designated contacts of the Licensee.\n8.2 New releases are made available to all Licences held; the Licensee may continue to use the previous major release for 12 months after a new major release.\n\n9. Data protection\n9.1 Login records processed by Tessellate for licence management are processed as a processor on behalf of the Licensee; they are deleted 24 months after collection.\n\n======================================================================\nB. AMENDMENTS\n======================================================================\nAmendment No. 1 (signed 12 February 2024)\nB1.1 Named Users who, on the Measurement Date, have been on continuous leave of absence (parental, sick, sabbatical) for 90 days or more, and whose accounts were not used during that leave, are not counted as Named Users for that Measurement Date.\nB1.2 The Licensee may transfer a Licence from one individual to another no more than once per quarter per Licence.\n\nAmendment No. 2 (signed 20 January 2026, effective 1 March 2026)\nB2.1 The Licensee may register one or more Non-Production Environments (test, QA, training) with Tessellate by submitting form NPE-1. Registration takes effect on the date Tessellate confirms receipt of form NPE-1.\nB2.2 Accounts that exist only in a registered Non-Production Environment are not counted in the true-up for any Measurement Date on which the registration was in effect.\nB2.3 Accounts in an environment that is not registered on the Measurement Date are counted in the same way as Production Environment accounts, and each individual is counted once across all environments (1.4).\nB2.4 Non-Production Environments may not be used for production design work; misuse ends the registration retroactively.\n\n======================================================================\nC. ORDER FORMS\n======================================================================\nOF-1 (1 March 2023): 100 Licences, term 1 March 2023 – 28 February 2028.\nOF-2 draft v1 (circulated 10 January 2025, not signed): 20 additional Licences from 1 March 2025.\nOF-2 (signed 18 February 2025): 15 additional Licences, licence start date 1 March 2025, co-terminous with OF-1.\nOF-3 (signed 28 August 2026): 10 additional Licences, licence start date 1 September 2026, co-terminous with OF-1. Purchase order PO-26-4471 issued 27 August 2026.\n\n======================================================================\nD. ACCOUNT INVENTORY AS OF 31 AUGUST 2026 (identity management export + Tessellate admin console)\n======================================================================\nD1. Production tenant \"kestrel-prod\":\n  D1.1 Enabled named accounts (employees of Kestrel Marine Systems AS and its affiliate Kestrel Offshore Design AB): 96 accounts. Identity management shows that two engineers each hold two accounts (a standard account and an \"-admin\" account): j.moreau / jmoreau-admin; s.halvorsen / shalvorsen-admin. All other accounts belong to different individuals.\n  D1.2 One of the 96 accounts belongs to an engineer on parental leave since 15 June 2026 (account not used since 12 June 2026).\n  D1.3 Disabled accounts (still present in the tenant): 3 accounts disabled on 20 August 2026 (leavers: last working day 19 August 2026); 4 accounts disabled on 15 July 2026 (leavers). None of these seven individuals has any other account.\n  D1.4 Shared account \"cad-kiosk-01\" (workshop terminal, hull fabrication hall): login audit (badge-to-terminal records) for 1 June – 31 August 2026 shows use by 6 distinct individuals, all shop-floor technicians. None of them holds a named account.\n  D1.5 Contractor accounts: 8 accounts for staff of Pellam Engineering GmbH (piping design outsourcing, framework contract FC-2024-09). Pellam holds its own Tessellate subscription for its other customers; no written confirmation from Tessellate under 3.2 is on file. The 8 Pellam individuals hold no other Kestrel accounts.\n  D1.6 Service accounts svc-render-1, svc-render-2, svc-render-3 (rendering server). Admin console: svc-render-1 and svc-render-3: API sessions only. svc-render-2: API sessions, plus 14 interactive desktop-client logins between 3 and 21 August 2026 from workstation WS-HULL-117, assigned to a.lindqvist (summer intern, employed 1 June – 28 August 2026; no named Tessellate account; intern's Windows account disabled 28 August 2026).\nD2. QA tenant \"kestrel-qa\" (set up 1 April 2026 for plug-in testing):\n  D2.1 9 accounts. Four of them belong to engineers who also hold accounts in kestrel-prod (counted in D1.1). The other five belong to software testers who hold no other Tessellate account.\n  D2.2 Form NPE-1 for \"kestrel-qa\" submitted by IT on 2 September 2026; Tessellate confirmed receipt on 5 September 2026.\nD3. Training tenant \"kestrel-train\" (registered Non-Production Environment, Tessellate confirmation of NPE-1 dated 14 March 2026): 12 accounts, all belonging to individuals who also hold kestrel-prod accounts.\nD4. Web viewer guest links (read-only, no login): 41 links created in 2026. The web viewer's guest mode is licensed separately under the \"Viewer Unlimited\" add-on and does not require Named User Licences.\n\nD6. Sample rows from the identity management export (format illustration; full export attached to the worksheet):\n  j.moreau | kestrel-prod | enabled | employee | last login 2026-08-31\n  jmoreau-admin | kestrel-prod | enabled | employee (admin role) | last login 2026-08-12\n  k.ostby | kestrel-prod | disabled 2026-08-20 | leaver | last login 2026-08-19\n  r.vinter | kestrel-prod | disabled 2026-07-15 | leaver | last login 2026-07-14\n  cad-kiosk-01 | kestrel-prod | enabled | shared | last login 2026-08-31\n  p.gruber.pellam | kestrel-prod | enabled | contractor (Pellam) | last login 2026-08-28\n  svc-render-2 | kestrel-prod | enabled | service | last API call 2026-08-31, last interactive login 2026-08-21\n  t.nyberg | kestrel-qa | enabled | tester | last login 2026-08-30\n\nD5. Identity management notes:\n  D5.1 Leavers' accounts are disabled on the working day after the last working day and deleted after 90 days.\n  D5.2 Contractors' accounts are created on request of the Kestrel project manager and reviewed quarterly; last review 30 June 2026 (all 8 Pellam accounts confirmed as needed).\n  D5.3 Engineers returning from leave are re-enabled automatically; the account of the engineer on parental leave (D1.2) was never disabled.\n  D5.4 The kiosk terminal was replaced on 1 September 2026 by three named-login terminals; cad-kiosk-01 was disabled on 1 September 2026.\n\n======================================================================\nE. PREVIOUS TRUE-UP\n======================================================================\nTrue-Up Report 2025 (Measurement Date 31 August 2025): Named Users reported 109; Licences held 115; no shortfall. The 2025 report counted cad-kiosk-01 as one Named User and did not include contractor accounts (Pellam's framework contract started in October 2025 for piping design; the earlier Pellam engagement used Pellam's own premises and licences).\nIT Asset Management working assumption for 2026 (from the 2025 method sheet): \"count enabled named accounts in kestrel-prod, plus shared accounts as one each; test tenants excluded\".\n"}
{"expected": 1, "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-08", "id": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-08", "labels": ["0", "1", "2", "3"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3016, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Affected tenants are those above 5 % failures in a window (2.2): 318 of 4,020 active tenants = 7.9 % -> SEV-3 range (the 455 with any failure would give 11.3 %). Enterprise tenants: T-12977 is a trial (2.3) and Harwell was not an affected tenant, so 2 -> SEV-3, not the 3+ SEV-2 criterion. Invoice API is not payment processing (2.4), so neither SEV-1 nor the CN-5.1.2 floor applies. Webhook events were replayed automatically and 11 min <= 15 min: recoverable within RPO, no floor. Base SEV-3. Duration is 13:40 to 17:40 = exactly 4 h, not more than 4 h, so no raise (the 4 h 10 min alert-to-all-clear is not the duration). The v1 Workaround was available to all affected tenants from 14:08, i.e. for the whole duration after the first 30 minutes (from 14:10) -> lower to SEV-4 (4.2), but CN-5.1.1 floors any incident with an affected Enterprise tenant at SEV-3. The routine security review is not a security incident (S4). Final: SEV-3.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "2", "why_hard": "Each of five criteria (affected-tenant definition, trial exclusion, payment-processing scope, RPO, duration boundary) plus a workaround reduction and an amendment floor must be applied in order."}, "question": {"criteria": ["SEV-4 (lowest)", "SEV-3", "SEV-2", "SEV-1 (highest)"], "instructions": "What final severity must the post-incident review assign to INC-2026-0908-114 under ISR v5.0 as amended by CN-5.1? Output the level index (0 = SEV-4 ... 3 = SEV-1).", "type": "score"}, "split": "public", "state": "LATTICEPAY PLATFORM — INCIDENT MANAGEMENT\nPost-incident severity review for INC-2026-0908-114 (\"Invoice API elevated 5xx, eu-west\")\nPack contents: (A) Incident Severity Runbook ISR, version 5.0, with Change Notice CN-5.1; (B) Security Incident Classification Standard SICS-2 (extract); (C) glossary extract from the Platform Handbook; (D) incident timeline and monitoring data; (E) customer impact data; (F) on-call notes.\n\n======================================================================\n(A) INCIDENT SEVERITY RUNBOOK ISR v5.0 (effective 1 February 2026)\n======================================================================\n1. Purpose\n1.1 The severity of an incident determines paging, communication cadence, executive notification and the post-incident review format. The final severity is set at the post-incident review according to this runbook, based on the facts established after the incident; the severity declared during the incident is provisional.\n1.2 This runbook covers availability, performance and data incidents. Security incidents (suspected or confirmed unauthorised access, data exfiltration, credential compromise) are classified under SICS-2 instead; where an incident is both, the higher of the two severities applies.\n\n2. Definitions\n2.1 \"Active tenant\" — a tenant that sent at least one API request in the 7 days before the incident started.\n2.2 \"Affected tenant\" — an active tenant for which more than 5 % of its API requests failed (5xx or timeout) in at least one 10-minute monitoring window during the incident. Tenants with some failed requests below that rate in every window are not affected tenants.\n2.3 \"Enterprise tenant\" — a tenant on an Enterprise plan with a signed Enterprise Master Services Agreement. Tenants on an Enterprise Trial (evaluation) plan are not Enterprise tenants for the purposes of this runbook.\n2.4 \"Payment processing\" — the authorisation, capture, refund and payout APIs, and the ledger writes behind them. Invoicing, billing documents (invoice PDFs, statements), reporting and the merchant dashboard are not payment processing.\n2.5 \"Incident duration\" — the time from the start of the first 10-minute monitoring window in which the incident's failure criterion was met to the end of the last such window. Alerting, acknowledgement and verification times are not part of the incident duration.\n2.6 \"Workaround\" — a documented alternative that allows affected tenants to complete the affected operation without LatticePay intervention (e.g. a fallback endpoint), communicated to them on the status page or directly.\n2.7 \"Data loss\" — loss of data that LatticePay had acknowledged as received. Data that can be restored automatically from the write-ahead log or replayed from the event archive, without manual reconstruction and with no more than 15 minutes of data affected, is \"recoverable within RPO\".\n\n3. Base severity (impact)\n3.1 SEV-1: more than 40 % of active tenants affected; or any unrecoverable data loss; or complete unavailability of payment processing in any region for 5 minutes or more.\n3.2 SEV-2: more than 10 % and up to 40 % of active tenants affected; or 3 or more Enterprise tenants affected.\n3.3 SEV-3: at least 1 % and up to 10 % of active tenants affected; or 1 or 2 Enterprise tenants affected.\n3.4 SEV-4: fewer than 1 % of active tenants affected and no Enterprise tenant affected.\n3.5 Where several criteria apply, the highest resulting severity is the base severity.\n\n4. Adjustments (applied to the base severity in this order)\n4.1 Duration: if the incident duration is more than 4 hours, raise the severity by one level (SEV-4 → SEV-3, SEV-3 → SEV-2, SEV-2 → SEV-1).\n4.2 Workaround: if a Workaround was available to all affected tenants for the whole incident duration after the first 30 minutes, lower the severity by one level.\n4.3 An adjustment never moves the severity above SEV-1 or below SEV-4.\n\n5. Communication by severity\n5.1 SEV-1: status page within 15 minutes; updates every 30 minutes; executive on-call paged; customer RCA within 5 business days.\n5.2 SEV-2: status page within 30 minutes; updates hourly; customer RCA within 10 business days.\n5.3 SEV-3: status page if more than 20 tenants affected; internal review only.\n5.4 SEV-4: no status page; ticket-level follow-up.\n\n6. Roles\n6.1 Incident commander (IC): declares provisional severity, coordinates responders.\n6.2 Communications lead: status page and Enterprise account managers.\n6.3 Scribe: timeline in the incident channel.\n6.4 Service owner: confirms the technical facts used for the final severity (affected tenants, duration, data impact) in writing before the review.\n6.5 The IC's provisional severity is not changed during the incident for reclassification purposes; reclassification happens only at the review.\n\n7. Post-incident review\n7.1 A post-incident review is held within 5 business days for SEV-1 and SEV-2 incidents and within 15 business days for SEV-3. SEV-4 incidents are reviewed in the weekly operations meeting.\n7.2 The review records the final severity, the facts on which it is based and the corrective actions.\n7.3 Final severity drives the reliability scorecard: SEV-1 = 10 points, SEV-2 = 5, SEV-3 = 2, SEV-4 = 0.\n\n8. Historical note\n8.1 ISR v4.2 (in force until 31 January 2026) used \"tenants with any failed request\" as affected tenants and treated Enterprise Trial tenants as Enterprise tenants. It had no workaround adjustment and no floors.\n\nAppendix — Recent final severities (for consistency checks)\nINC-2026-0114-021 | ISR v4.2 | 212 of 3,870 tenants with any failure (5.5 %) | 1 Enterprise | SEV-3\nINC-2026-0322-067 | ISR v5.0 | 44 affected of 3,950 (1.1 %) | 0 Enterprise | 2 h 10 min | Workaround from 00:12 | SEV-4\nINC-2026-0611-088 | ISR v5.0 + CN-5.1 | Payouts API degraded 22 min, 0.8 % affected | SEV-2 (payment processing floor)\nINC-2026-0729-102 | ISR v5.0 + CN-5.1 | 9.4 % affected | 1 Enterprise | 4 h 30 min | SEV-2 (duration raise)\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nCHANGE NOTICE CN-5.1 (approved by the Reliability Council, effective 1 June 2026)\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\nCN-5.1.1 New section 4.4: \"Floor for Enterprise impact. After the adjustments in 4.1 and 4.2, an incident in which at least one Enterprise tenant was affected is never below SEV-3.\"\nCN-5.1.2 New section 3.6: \"Payment processing floor. Any incident in which payment processing was degraded (more than 5 % failed requests in a 10-minute window) for 15 minutes or more is at least SEV-2 before adjustments.\"\nCN-5.1.3 Section 2.7 is amended by adding: \"Data that is recoverable only by manual reconstruction, or where more than 15 minutes of data is affected, is recoverable but not within RPO; such an incident is at least SEV-2 before adjustments.\"\nCN-5.1.4 Section 5.3 is amended: status page for SEV-3 if more than 10 tenants affected.\n\n======================================================================\n(B) SECURITY INCIDENT CLASSIFICATION STANDARD SICS-2 — EXTRACT\n======================================================================\nS1 Any confirmed unauthorised access to cardholder data: S-1 (maps to SEV-1).\nS2 Suspected unauthorised access under investigation: S-2 (maps to SEV-2) until closed.\nS3 Vulnerability discovered without evidence of exploitation: S-3 (maps to SEV-3).\nS4 A security review opened as routine follow-up to an availability incident, where no unauthorised access is suspected, is not a security incident.\n\n======================================================================\n(C) PLATFORM HANDBOOK — GLOSSARY EXTRACT\n======================================================================\nInvoice API — generates invoice documents (PDF/UBL) for merchants' customers and stores them in the document store. Part of the Billing Documents product.\nPayments API — authorisation, capture, refund; Payouts API — merchant settlements.\nWebhook dispatcher — delivers event notifications to tenants; events are persisted in the event archive for 30 days and can be replayed automatically.\nRegions: eu-west (primary for EU tenants), eu-central (DR).\n\n======================================================================\n(D) INCIDENT TIMELINE (UTC) AND MONITORING\n======================================================================\n8 September 2026\n13:31 Deployment of document-store client library 4.12.0 to Invoice API pods in eu-west.\n13:40–13:50 First 10-minute monitoring window in which the Invoice API 5xx rate exceeded 5 % for affected tenants.\n13:41 Alert \"invoice-api 5xx > 2 %\" fired; on-call acknowledged 13:44.\n13:52 IC declared provisional SEV-2 (\"wide customer impact, cause unknown, payments?\").\n14:05 Payments API, Payouts API: error rates checked — normal (0.02 %); no degradation of authorisation, capture or refund at any time during the incident.\n14:08 Workaround published on the status page and sent to all tenants with failed requests: \"Use the v1 invoice endpoint /v1/invoices/render (synchronous) until further notice.\" The v1 endpoint remained fully available throughout.\n14:47 Webhook dispatcher backlog; 11 minutes of \"invoice.rendered\" events (14:36–14:47) dropped from the in-memory queue during a pod restart.\n15:10 Event replay from the event archive started automatically for 14:36–14:47; completed 15:18; no manual steps.\n16:55 Rollback to library 4.11.3 started.\n17:30–17:40 Last 10-minute monitoring window with 5xx rate above 5 %.\n17:51 IC declared all-clear after verification.\nSecurity: on 9 September a routine security review ticket SEC-REV-2231 was opened \"as standard follow-up to any library-related incident\"; no unauthorised access suspected; closed 11 September with no findings.\n\n======================================================================\n(E) CUSTOMER IMPACT DATA (analytics export, 10 September 2026)\n======================================================================\nActive tenants (at least one request in the 7 days before 13:31 on 8 September): 4,020. Provisioned tenants (all, including dormant): 5,610.\nTenants with at least one failed Invoice API request during the incident: 455.\nTenants with more than 5 % of API requests failed in at least one 10-minute window: 318.\nEnterprise-plan tenants among the 318:\n  T-10044 Moravia Retail Group — Enterprise plan, Enterprise MSA signed 2023.\n  T-11820 Kestrel Hospitality — Enterprise plan, Enterprise MSA signed March 2026.\n  T-12977 Orinoco Sports GmbH — Enterprise Trial plan (evaluation until 30 September 2026); MSA under negotiation.\nEnterprise-plan tenants among the 137 tenants with failures below 5 % in every window: T-10390 Harwell Foods (Enterprise, MSA 2024).\nPayments API volume during the incident: normal; no tenant had payment failures above baseline.\n\n======================================================================\n(F) ON-CALL NOTES\n======================================================================\nIC handover note (18:05): \"Total impact roughly 4 h 10 min from first alert to all-clear. Three Enterprise customers affected, 455 tenants saw errors. Webhook events lost for 11 min but replayed. Suggest keeping SEV-2 for the review.\"\nCommunications lead: status page updates posted 14:02, 14:08, 15:00, 16:00, 17:00, 17:55. Account managers of T-10044, T-11820 and T-12977 informed.\nRoot-cause summary (engineering, 10 September): \"Library 4.12.0 changed the default connection-pool size for the document store from 64 to 8. Under normal afternoon load, render requests queued and timed out after 30 s, returning 504. The synchronous v1 endpoint uses a separate pool and was unaffected. The in-memory webhook queue lost events on the pod restart at 14:47 because the graceful-shutdown hook was also affected; the archive-based replay worked as designed.\"\nCorrective actions proposed: pin pool size in configuration; canary deployments for library upgrades; alert threshold review.\nReliability Council note (review agenda for 15 September): INC-2026-0908-114 — final severity to be determined per ISR v5.0 as amended.\n"}
{"expected": "credit_minus_restocking_fee", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-10", "id": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-10", "labels": ["full_credit", "credit_minus_restocking_fee", "repair_or_replace_only", "reject_return"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3021, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The CX-400s were configured in the online configurator with catalogue option codes only (no X- code), so under Amendment 1 they are Standard Products (the old CRP-4 rule and the other order with X-CC do not apply). The RMA Request Date is 10 Sep (the incomplete 9 Sep draft does not count) and the Delivery Date is 12 Aug per proof of delivery, so 29 days: within 30 (dispatch/invoice 7 Aug would give 34). Inspection found NFF, so the units are not Defective (2.5, 5.4) but are Unused (31 h < 72 h, no damage), so they are returnable for credit under 3.1. The restocking fee applies unless waived: the tier on the RMA Request Date is Silver (recalculated 1 July 2026), the return is not due to a Corvid error, and the product is not discontinued. Result: credit less 15 %.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "full_credit", "why_hard": "Product classification depends on an amendment, the window on the Delivery Date definition, the NFF result removes the defect route, and the tier fell from Gold one quarter earlier."}, "question": {"criteria": {"credit_minus_restocking_fee": "Accept the return and credit the invoiced net value less the 15 % restocking fee.", "full_credit": "Accept the return and credit the full invoiced net value without a restocking fee.", "reject_return": "Reject the return; no credit, repair or replacement.", "repair_or_replace_only": "No credit; the units are to be repaired or replaced."}, "instructions": "What decision must the returns desk issue on RQ-26-09-3318 under CRP-5 as amended?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "CORVID INDUSTRIAL CONTROLS GMBH — CUSTOMER SERVICE / RETURNS DESK\nReturn authorisation assessment, RMA request RQ-26-09-3318\nPack: (1) Commercial Returns Policy CRP-5 (edition 1 January 2025) with Amendment 1; (2) extract from the superseded CRP-4; (3) Partner Programme rules (tier extract); (4) order, delivery and invoice records; (5) RMA request and inspection report; (6) account history.\n\n(1) COMMERCIAL RETURNS POLICY CRP-5\n\n§1 Scope\n1.1 CRP-5 applies to returns by business customers of products bought directly from Corvid. Products bought through distributors are returned to the distributor under the distributor's terms.\n1.2 Warranty claims under the Corvid Limited Warranty (24 months) are handled separately by the Service department; a return under CRP-5 is a commercial return (return for credit) and does not affect warranty rights.\n\n§2 Definitions\n2.1 \"Standard Product\" — a product listed in the Corvid catalogue, supplied in a catalogue configuration.\n2.2 \"Custom-built Product\" — a product built to the customer's specification, including any product whose configuration was not available in the catalogue, products with customer-specific firmware, labelling or housings, and products ordered under a project quotation.\n2.3 \"Delivery Date\" — the date on which the goods were delivered to the customer's delivery address, as shown by the carrier's proof of delivery. The dispatch date and the invoice date are not the Delivery Date.\n2.4 \"RMA Request Date\" — the date on which the customer submits a complete return request in the Corvid partner portal (order number, items, quantities and reason).\n2.5 \"Defective\" — a product in which Corvid's inspection confirms a fault in materials or workmanship present at delivery. A product returned as defective in which the inspection finds no fault (\"No Fault Found\", NFF) is not Defective.\n2.6 \"Unused\" — not installed in a powered application for more than 72 hours in total, with no physical damage, and returned with all accessories.\n\n§3 Return window and eligibility\n3.1 Standard Products may be returned for credit if the RMA Request Date is no later than 30 days after the Delivery Date and the products are Unused, or are Defective.\n3.2 Custom-built Products are not returnable for credit. Defective Custom-built Products are repaired or replaced under 5.3.\n3.3 Returns of Standard Products requested more than 30 days after the Delivery Date are rejected, unless the products are Defective (5.3 applies).\n3.4 Minimum return value: EUR 100 per RMA.\n\n§4 Credit\n4.1 Returns accepted under 3.1 are credited at the invoiced net price.\n4.2 A restocking fee of 15 % of the credited value is deducted for returns that are not Defective.\n4.3 The restocking fee is waived (a) for Gold partners; (b) where the return results from a Corvid shipping or order-entry error; (c) for products discontinued by Corvid within 60 days before the RMA Request Date.\n4.4 Freight for returns that are not Defective is paid by the customer.\n4.5 No restocking fee may be waived other than under 4.3, except with the written approval of the Head of Customer Service.\n4.6 Credits are calculated per RMA; partial returns of an order line are credited pro rata.\n\n§5 Defective products\n5.1 Products returned as Defective are inspected within 10 business days of receipt.\n5.2 Defective Standard Products returned within the 30-day window are credited in full (no restocking fee); outside the window they are repaired or replaced.\n5.3 Defective Custom-built Products are repaired or replaced; no credit is given.\n5.4 NFF returns are treated as returns that are not Defective. Eligibility and credit are assessed under §3 and §4 as at the RMA Request Date.\n\n§6 Process\n6.1 Goods must not be sent back without an RMA number.\n6.2 The returns desk issues its decision (credit with or without fee, repair/replace, or rejection) after inspection.\n6.3 Goods returned without an RMA number, or more than 15 days after the RMA number was issued, may be refused at the warehouse.\n6.4 The customer bears the risk of loss in transit for returns that are not Defective.\n\n§7 Special cases\n7.1 Demonstration units lent to customers are not sales and are returned under the loan agreement.\n7.2 Trade-in programme: customers replacing CX-200 controllers with CX-400 controllers may return the old units for a trade-in credit of EUR 150 per unit within 90 days of the CX-400 delivery; trade-ins are not returns under CRP-5.\n7.3 Software licences and firmware upgrade keys are not returnable once activated.\n7.4 Spare parts ordered through the Service department are returned under the Service terms.\n\n§8 Credit notes\n8.1 Credit notes are issued in the currency of the original invoice within 10 business days of the returns desk decision.\n8.2 Credit notes are offset against open invoices first; any balance is paid out on request.\n8.3 Restocking fees are shown as a separate line on the credit note.\n\n§9 Returns from outside the EU\n9.1 Customers outside the EU must return goods with export documentation marking them as returned goods; customs duties charged on re-import are deducted from the credit unless the return is Defective.\n9.2 Norway: returns are processed with a T1 transit document; no import duty is charged on returned Corvid goods of EU origin if the documentation is correct.\n\n§10 Relationship with the Limited Warranty\n10.1 A customer whose return request is rejected under §3 keeps any warranty rights for Defective products.\n10.2 Products found NFF under a warranty claim are returned to the customer at the customer's cost; an inspection fee of EUR 60 per unit may be charged under the Service terms.\n\nAMENDMENT 1 TO CRP-5 (effective 1 April 2026)\nA1.1 Section 2.1 is supplemented: \"Products configured by the customer in the Corvid online configurator using only catalogue option codes are Standard Products. A configuration that includes any option code with the prefix X- (special engineering option) makes the product a Custom-built Product.\"\nA1.2 Section 2.2: the words \"including any product whose configuration was not available in the catalogue\" are deleted.\nA1.3 Section 4.3(c): \"60 days\" is replaced by \"90 days\".\nA1.4 New section 3.5: \"Where a customer submits several RMA requests for one sales order, each request is assessed separately on its own RMA Request Date.\"\nA1.5 Sections 4.2 and 5.2 are otherwise unchanged.\n\n(2) SUPERSEDED — CRP-4 (1 January 2021 – 31 December 2024), EXTRACT\n\"§3.1 Return window: 14 days from invoice date. §4.2 Restocking fee 20 %. §4.3 Fee waived for Gold partners and for Corvid errors. §2.2 Any product assembled to order, including configurator orders, is a Custom-built Product. §5.2 Defective Standard Products: credited in full within 14 days of invoice date, otherwise repaired or replaced.\"\n\n(3) PARTNER PROGRAMME — TIER RULES (EXTRACT)\nP1 Tiers: Gold (trailing 12-month net purchases of EUR 100,000 or more), Silver (EUR 25,000 to 99,999.99), Registered (below EUR 25,000).\nP2 Tiers are recalculated on the first day of each calendar quarter (1 January, 1 April, 1 July, 1 October), based on net purchases in the 12 full calendar months before that day. The new tier applies from that day until the next recalculation.\nP3 Where a CRP-5 rule depends on the tier, the tier on the RMA Request Date applies.\nP4 Gold partners receive a 3 % volume rebate on the following quarter's purchases.\nP5 Gold partners have access to priority technical support (4-hour response) and free training places (two per year).\nP6 Partners are informed of a tier change by e-mail within 10 business days after recalculation; the tier change is effective from the recalculation date even if the e-mail is sent later.\nP7 Tier status is displayed in the partner portal header; the display is refreshed monthly.\n\n(4) ORDER, DELIVERY AND INVOICE RECORDS\nCustomer: Halvard Automation AS (customer no. 20-4471), direct customer.\nSales order SO-26-18830, placed 28 July 2026 via the online configurator:\n  12 × CX-400 PLC controller, configuration code CX400-IO16-PS24-FWSTD-HSG2 (options: IO-16 digital I/O; PS-24 24 V supply; FW-STD standard firmware; HSG-2 DIN-rail housing). All option codes are catalogue codes; no X- codes.\n  Net price EUR 1,180.00 per unit; total EUR 14,160.00.\nDispatched from Corvid warehouse Kassel: 7 August 2026. Invoice INV-26-55190 dated 7 August 2026.\nCarrier proof of delivery (NordLink Freight): delivered and signed for at Halvard Automation, Stavanger, 12 August 2026, 10:41.\nSales order SO-26-17702, placed 2 July 2026 (same customer): 4 × CX-400 with option X-CC (conformal coating, special engineering option), delivered 20 July 2026 — not part of this RMA.\n\n(5) RMA REQUEST AND INSPECTION\nRMA request RQ-26-09-3318 submitted in the partner portal on 10 September 2026, 16:05 (complete): SO-26-18830, 12 × CX-400, reason \"Defective — units reboot randomly under load\".\nEarlier incomplete portal draft saved 9 September 2026 (no items selected; not submitted).\nGoods received at Kassel 17 September 2026 in original packaging, all accessories present; installation log in the units' memory shows 31 powered hours in total per unit.\nInspection report IR-26-3318 (22 September 2026): all 12 units tested for 48 h under full load; no reboots; the customer's project file set the watchdog timeout to 50 ms (default 500 ms), which causes resets under load. Result: No Fault Found on all units. No physical damage.\nCustomer e-mail (23 September 2026): \"Understood about the watchdog setting. We would still like to return the units because the project was cancelled. As a long-standing Gold partner we expect the usual full credit.\"\n\n(6) ACCOUNT HISTORY — HALVARD AUTOMATION AS\nTier change e-mail \"Your partner tier from 1 July 2026: Silver\" sent 9 July 2026 to the purchasing contact. Portal header on 10 September 2026 displayed: \"Partner tier: Gold\" (display last refreshed 30 June 2026; the monthly refresh job has failed since July 2026, IT ticket IT-26-7710 open). Tier master data in the ERP (customer 20-4471, field \"Partner tier\"): Silver, valid from 1 July 2026; previous value Gold, valid 1 October 2024 – 30 June 2026.\nTier history: Gold from 1 October 2024; recalculation 1 July 2026 (net purchases July 2025 – June 2026: EUR 96,400.00) → Silver from 1 July 2026. Next recalculation 1 October 2026 (net purchases October 2025 – September 2026 will include SO-26-17702 and SO-26-18830).\nNet purchases by month (EUR, invoiced, net of credit notes): Jul 2025 6,120; Aug 2025 9,870; Sep 2025 4,300; Oct 2025 11,450; Nov 2025 8,200; Dec 2025 2,150; Jan 2026 7,940; Feb 2026 10,330; Mar 2026 12,060; Apr 2026 5,480; May 2026 9,700; Jun 2026 8,800 (total July 2025 – June 2026: 96,400.00); Jul 2026 5,720 (SO-26-17702); Aug 2026 14,160 (SO-26-18830).\nCustomer master search \"Halvard\" also returns: Halvard Automation GmbH (customer no. 20-5102, Hamburg; separate legal entity of the same group; own account; Gold partner since 1 January 2026, trailing purchases EUR 212,000). Tiers are held per customer account.\nCustoms: RQ-26-09-3318 goods shipped back from Stavanger under T1 transit document TR-26-NO-88412; no duty charged.\nPrevious RMAs: RQ-25-11-2210 (2025, 3 × CX-200, returned within 14 days, Gold partner, full credit, no fee).\nCorvid catalogue status: CX-400 current product; no discontinuation announced.\nOpen items: none; payment terms 30 days net; INV-26-55190 paid 4 September 2026.\n\n(7) OTHER CORRESPONDENCE\nAccount manager (Corvid, Stavanger territory) to Halvard Automation AS, 11 September 2026: \"Thanks for the RMA. Once inspection is done we'll sort out the credit — as always for you, I'll make sure there are no surprises.\" No approval by the Head of Customer Service is recorded on RQ-26-09-3318.\nService department note (22 September 2026): \"Recommend customer applies application note AN-400-07 (watchdog configuration) for future projects.\"\nPortal help text (configurator page): \"Configurations built from catalogue options ship within 5 business days. Special engineering options (X- codes) require a project quotation and extend lead time to 6–8 weeks.\"\n"}
{"expected": "no", "family": "long_policy", "group": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-11", "id": "hard-opus-c-long_policy-11", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 3013, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "She will be E5 on the start date, so under A1 four Years = 4 x 365 = 1,460 days of Qualifying Service are needed. The 2021-22 Analytics service is not reinstated: she was re-hired by a different group company (Tidemark Data Services), C3; the move to Analytics in 2024 was a no-break transfer, so service runs from 15 Aug 2022 (C2). 15 Aug 2022 to 31 Jan 2027 = 1,631 days. Paid maternity leave counts (C4), but the unpaid parental leave 1 Jun-22 Nov 2024 is one continuous period of 175 days (> 90), excluded in full (C5): 1,631 - 175 = 1,456 < 1,460. Ratings, notice, no PIP and no previous sabbatical are fine, but Qualifying Service falls 4 days short. Reinstating 2021 service, or deducting only the 85 days above 90, would make her eligible.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "yes", "why_hard": "Needs group-transfer vs re-hire continuity rules, the grade-at-start-date amendment, and a whole-period exclusion of long unpaid leave, landing a few days short of 1,460."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "At least one eligibility condition is not met.", "true": "All eligibility conditions in SAB-2 3.1 (as amended) and 3.2 are met."}, "instructions": "Is employee TA-10388 eligible for the requested sabbatical starting 1 February 2027 under SAB-2 as amended?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "TIDEMARK GROUP — PEOPLE OPERATIONS (UK)\nSabbatical application review: SAB-APP-2026-0219\nPack: (1) Sabbatical Leave Policy SAB-2 (issued 1 March 2024) with Amendment 2026/1; (2) Continuity of Service Guideline CSG-1 (extracts); (3) Career Break Policy CB-1 (extract, for comparison); (4) employee records; (5) application and manager's endorsement.\n\n(1) SABBATICAL LEAVE POLICY SAB-2\n\n1. Purpose\n1.1 Sabbatical leave gives long-serving employees a period of paid leave for rest, study or voluntary work. It is granted once in any seven-year period.\n\n2. Definitions\n2.1 \"Employer\" — the Tidemark Group company that employs the employee on the date of application.\n2.2 \"Qualifying Service\" — service counted in accordance with the Continuity of Service Guideline CSG-1, as at the day before the requested sabbatical start date.\n2.3 \"Year\" — for the purposes of this policy, a period of 365 days of Qualifying Service. Qualifying Service is counted in calendar days.\n2.4 \"Grade\" — the employee's grade in the Tidemark grading structure (E1 to E9).\n2.5 \"Performance rating\" — the rating from the annual performance cycle (Outstanding, Strong, Meets, Partly meets, Does not meet).\n\n3. Eligibility\n3.1 An employee is eligible for sabbatical leave if, on the requested sabbatical start date:\n  (a) the employee has at least five Years of Qualifying Service;\n  (b) the employee's two most recent completed performance ratings are \"Meets\" or better;\n  (c) the employee is not subject to a formal performance improvement plan or a live disciplinary warning; and\n  (d) the employee has not taken a sabbatical in the previous seven years.\n3.2 The application must be submitted at least three months before the requested start date.\n3.3 The employee's line manager must endorse the application; the manager may propose a different start date for business reasons, but may not refuse an eligible application more than once.\n\n4. Duration and pay\n4.1 Sabbatical leave is four weeks on full pay, which may be combined with up to four weeks of annual leave.\n4.2 During sabbatical leave, all contractual benefits continue.\n4.3 Sabbatical pay is calculated on basic salary only; allowances for on-call or shift work are not included.\n\n5. Return\n5.1 An employee who resigns within six months of returning from sabbatical repays the sabbatical pay.\n5.2 On return, the employee resumes the same role on the same terms.\n5.3 An employee may be asked to share what they learned during a study sabbatical with their team.\n\n6. Process\n6.1 Applications are submitted through the HR portal (form SAB-A) with the requested start date, duration and purpose.\n6.2 People Operations checks eligibility within 15 working days and informs the employee and manager of the outcome.\n6.3 Where People Operations finds the employee not yet eligible, it states the earliest date on which the employee would become eligible, and the employee may re-apply for a start date on or after that date without further delay beyond 3.2.\n6.4 Approved sabbaticals may be postponed once by the employee, by up to six months, without re-application.\n\n7. Interaction with other leave\n7.1 Sabbatical leave cannot be combined with unpaid leave or a career break under CB-1.\n7.2 If the employee falls ill during sabbatical leave, the sabbatical is not extended.\n7.3 Public holidays falling within sabbatical leave are part of the sabbatical.\n\n8. Appeals\n8.1 An employee may appeal against a refusal to the Head of People Operations within 10 working days. The appeal is decided on the policy as written; discretionary exceptions are not available.\n\nAMENDMENT 2026/1 (approved by the Group People Committee, effective 1 January 2026)\nA1 Section 3.1(a) is replaced by: \"(a) the employee has at least five Years of Qualifying Service or, if the employee is in Grade E5 or above on the requested sabbatical start date, at least four Years of Qualifying Service;\".\nA2 Section 3.2: applications for start dates in December require four months' notice.\nA3 The amendment applies to applications submitted on or after 1 January 2026.\n\n(2) CONTINUITY OF SERVICE GUIDELINE CSG-1 — EXTRACTS\nC1 Qualifying Service is continuous employment with the Employer, counted from the start date of the current period of employment.\nC2 Transfers within the group: where an employee transfers from one Tidemark Group company to another without any break between the two employments, service with the previous company counts as service with the Employer.\nC3 Re-hire: where a former employee is re-hired by the same company within six months after the end of the previous employment, the previous period of employment is added to Qualifying Service (the gap between the employments does not count). Re-hire by a different Tidemark Group company does not reinstate the previous period.\nC4 Paid leave of any kind, including statutory maternity, paternity, adoption and shared parental leave while paid, and sickness absence, counts as Qualifying Service.\nC5 Unpaid leave: a single continuous period of unpaid leave of 90 days or fewer counts as Qualifying Service. A single continuous period of unpaid leave of more than 90 days does not break continuity, but the whole of that period is excluded from Qualifying Service.\nC6 Secondments to external organisations with a right to return count as Qualifying Service.\nC7 Periods on a zero-hours or casual contract with a group company before a permanent contract do not count.\nC8 Periods of notice worked count; periods of garden leave count.\nC9 Service with a company acquired by the group counts from the date of acquisition unless the acquisition agreement provides otherwise.\nC10 Industrial action days: excluded but do not break continuity.\nC11 Where an employee's contract is transferred by operation of law into a group company, service with the transferor counts.\nC12 Calculation example (days): an employee who started on 1 July 2021 and has a single unpaid leave of 30 days has, on 30 June 2025, 1,461 days of employment, all of which count.\n\n(3) CAREER BREAK POLICY CB-1 — EXTRACT (for comparison; not applicable to sabbatical leave)\nCB 2.1 Employees with three years of service may take an unpaid career break of 3 to 24 months. For the purposes of CB-1, service is counted from the first date of employment with any Tidemark Group company, including earlier periods of employment if re-hired within 12 months.\nCB 2.2 Career breaks do not count as service for any purpose.\n\n(4) EMPLOYEE RECORDS\nHR system search \"Rinaldi\" returned two records:\n  Record A — Marta Rinaldi, employee no. TA-10388, current Employer: Tidemark Analytics Ltd.\n    Employment history:\n      Tidemark Analytics Ltd, 1 March 2021 – 30 June 2022 (resigned; left to join a competitor).\n      Tidemark Data Services Ltd, 15 August 2022 – 31 December 2023 (new hire; employee no. TDS-2231).\n      Tidemark Analytics Ltd, from 1 January 2024 (transfer from Tidemark Data Services Ltd under the 2024 group reorganisation; no break; employee no. TA-10388).\n    Grade: E4 until 30 September 2026; E5 from 1 October 2026 (promotion letter dated 12 August 2026).\n    Leave history:\n      Statutory maternity leave, paid: 1 March 2024 – 31 May 2024.\n      Unpaid parental leave (extension, agreed with manager): 1 June 2024 – 22 November 2024.\n      Returned to work 25 November 2024 (Monday).\n      Annual leave and sickness absence: normal levels; no unpaid leave other than the above.\n    Payroll absence export (codes: MAT-P paid maternity; UPL unpaid leave; SICK sickness; AL annual leave), 2024:\n      MAT-P 2024-03-01 → 2024-05-31\n      UPL   2024-06-01 → 2024-11-22   (reason: parental, agreed extension; pension contributions suspended)\n      AL    2024-12-23 → 2024-12-31\n      SICK  2024-02-12 → 2024-02-14\n    Contract: permanent, full-time (37.5 h); location Bristol Harbourside office.\n    Performance ratings: 2024 cycle \"Strong\"; 2025 cycle \"Meets\"; 2026 cycle not yet completed.\n    Warnings / PIP: none. Previous sabbaticals: none.\n  Record B — Marta Rinaldi, employee no. TA-07712, Tidemark Analytics Ltd, Grade E6, continuous employment since 3 April 2018; sabbatical taken 2025.\n\n(5) APPLICATION\nApplication SAB-APP-2026-0219 submitted 15 September 2026 by employee no. TA-10388: \"Requested sabbatical: four weeks from Monday 1 February 2027, combined with two weeks of annual leave; purpose: completing a data-science certificate.\"\nLine manager's endorsement (18 September 2026): \"Fully support. Marta has been with Tidemark since 2021 and will be E5 by then.\"\nE-mail from the employee to People Operations (16 September 2026): \"For the service check — I first joined Tidemark in March 2021, so I'll have almost six years by February. I was away for a few months in 2022 but came back to the group.\"\nPension scheme record (Tidemark Group Pension Plan): pensionable service from 15 August 2022; contributions suspended 1 June – 22 November 2024.\nPeople Operations checklist: notice ≥ 3 months ✓ (15 Sep → 1 Feb); ratings ✓; PIP/warnings ✓; previous sabbatical ✓; Qualifying Service ☐ (to be completed by reviewer).\n\n(6) GROUP COMPANIES AND REORGANISATION NOTICE\nTidemark Group companies (UK): Tidemark Holdings plc; Tidemark Analytics Ltd; Tidemark Data Services Ltd (dissolved 30 June 2024 after the reorganisation); Tidemark Cloud Operations Ltd; Tidemark Insights Ltd (acquired 1 May 2023).\nReorganisation notice RN-2023-11 (issued 6 November 2023): \"With effect from 1 January 2024 all employees of Tidemark Data Services Ltd transfer to Tidemark Analytics Ltd on their existing terms. Continuous service with Tidemark Data Services Ltd is preserved for all purposes. Employee numbers change from TDS- to TA-.\"\nRecruitment file TDS-2231 (Tidemark Data Services Ltd, 2022): \"External candidate (previously employed by Tidemark Analytics Ltd until 30.06.2022). Offer 28.07.2022, start 15.08.2022. Probation 6 months.\"\n\n(7) GRADING STRUCTURE — EXTRACT\nE1–E3: associate and analyst roles. E4: senior analyst / senior engineer. E5: lead / principal individual contributor, or team manager. E6–E7: senior manager, head of function. E8–E9: director and executive.\nPromotions take effect on the date stated in the promotion letter. Grade changes are recorded in the HR system on their effective date.\n\n(8) SUPERSEDED — SABBATICAL POLICY SAB-1 (1 January 2019 – 29 February 2024), EXTRACT\n\"S1.1 Eligibility: seven years' continuous service with any Tidemark Group company, counted from the first day of employment; breaks of up to 12 months are bridged. S1.2 Sabbatical: up to eight weeks, of which four weeks paid. S1.3 Unpaid leave counts as service.\"\n\n(9) CORRESPONDENCE ON THE 2024 LEAVE\nLetter from the line manager to the employee, 20 May 2024: \"I'm happy to agree your request to extend your leave as unpaid parental leave from 1 June 2024, returning on Monday 25 November 2024. During this period you remain employed; your pension contributions and holiday accrual are suspended.\"\nLetter from People Operations, 22 May 2024: \"Your unpaid parental leave 1 June – 22 November 2024 has been recorded (code UPL). It does not break your continuous employment.\"\n\n(10) SAB-2 FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS (intranet, March 2024)\nQ: Can I work for another employer during my sabbatical? A: No. Voluntary work for a registered charity is allowed.\nQ: Can I split my sabbatical into two periods? A: No, sabbatical leave is taken as one continuous period.\nQ: Does sickness absence count towards my service? A: Yes, see CSG-1 C4.\nQ: I work part-time. Is my sabbatical shorter? A: No, it is four weeks; pay is at your contractual rate.\nQ: Can my manager refuse? A: Only once, for business reasons, if you are eligible (3.3).\nQ: When will I hear back? A: Within 15 working days of your application (6.2).\nQ: Does a sabbatical affect my bonus? A: No; bonus eligibility is assessed under the Bonus Plan, and sabbatical leave counts as time worked for bonus pro-rating.\nQ: Can I take a sabbatical immediately after returning from parental leave? A: Yes, if you are eligible; there is no minimum period back at work.\n"}
{"expected": "kuznets_technik", "family": "probability", "group": "hard-opus-c-probability-03", "id": "hard-opus-c-probability-03", "labels": ["halvorsen_polymer", "ortega_seal_works", "kuznets_technik"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 370, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "gold_probs": {"halvorsen_polymer": 0.1964, "kuznets_technik": 0.6027, "ortega_seal_works": 0.2009}, "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "P(supplier | failed with seal crack) is proportional to share x failure rate x seal-crack share: Halvorsen 0.55x0.008x0.20 = 0.00088; Ortega 0.30x0.030x0.10 = 0.00090; Kuznets 0.15x0.020x0.90 = 0.00270; total 0.00448. Posterior: Halvorsen 0.1964, Ortega 0.2009, Kuznets 0.6027. Ignoring the failure mode makes Ortega most likely (0.009/0.0164 = 0.55); share alone makes Halvorsen likeliest; the SC-K9 split and the all-parts scorecard are for other populations.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "ortega_seal_works", "why_hard": "Two Bayesian updates (failure, then failure mode) reverse the ranking suggested by either share or failure rate alone."}, "question": {"criteria": {"halvorsen_polymer": "The cartridge was supplied by Halvorsen Polymer AS.", "kuznets_technik": "The cartridge was supplied by Kuznets Technik GmbH.", "ortega_seal_works": "The cartridge was supplied by Ortega Seal Works S.L."}, "instructions": "Which supplier made the failed SC-K7 cartridge in FF-26-0412? Give probabilities that reflect the evidence in the state.", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "BRAEMONT PUMPS — FIELD FAILURE INVESTIGATION FF-26-0412\nFailed part: shaft seal cartridge, part number SC-K7, from a KX-40 circulation pump returned by a district-heating customer after 7 months in service. The supplier batch label was destroyed by heat; the cartridge body carries no supplier marking. Failure mode determined by the lab: radial seal-face crack (\"seal crack\").\n\nSupply data for SC-K7 (purchasing, deliveries into the KX-40 line for the relevant build period, January–June 2026):\n  Halvorsen Polymer AS ........ 55 % of SC-K7 cartridges\n  Ortega Seal Works S.L. ...... 30 %\n  Kuznets Technik GmbH ........ 15 %\n(For the SC-K9 cartridge used in the larger KX-60 pump, the split is Halvorsen 20 %, Ortega 50 %, Kuznets 30 %.)\n\nField failure rates of SC-K7 within 12 months in service (quality database, 2024–2026 builds, all failure modes):\n  Halvorsen 0.8 % · Ortega 3.0 % · Kuznets 2.0 %\nFailure-mode mix of those field failures (share of each supplier's SC-K7 failures that were seal cracks; the remainder were O-ring extrusion, spring fatigue or contamination):\n  Halvorsen 20 % · Ortega 10 % · Kuznets 90 %\nQuality engineer's note: failure rate and failure mode are recorded per supplier and are stable across build years; nothing else in the pump's history (installation, water chemistry within spec) distinguishes the suppliers.\nSupplier scorecard, 2025 (all parts supplied to Braemont, not only SC-K7): Halvorsen 98.9 % good, Ortega 96.1 %, Kuznets 99.2 %.\n"}
{"expected": "yes", "family": "temporal_numeric", "group": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-02", "id": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-02", "labels": ["no", "yes"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 562, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Actual elapsed times: Sat 28 Mar 09:00 EDT = 13:00 UTC; Sun 29 Mar 01:00 CET = 00:00 UTC, so the rest is exactly 11 h (4.1 met; assuming a 6-h NY-Berlin gap gives 10 h). The Berlin shift 01:00 CET to 12:00 CEST is 00:00-10:00 UTC = 10 h, not the nominal 11 h (4.2 met). The week totals 9+9+8+2+10+10 = 48 h, which does not exceed 48 (4.3 met). All other rests exceed 11 h. The rota tool's 49.0 uses wall-clock durations.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "no", "why_hard": "Three limits sit exactly at their thresholds only once both time zones' DST states are applied; wall-clock arithmetic breaches all three."}, "question": {"criteria": {"false": "At least one of 4.1, 4.2 or 4.3 is breached.", "true": "Every rest period is at least 11 h, no shift exceeds 10 h, and the rota week totals at most 48 h, all measured as actual elapsed time."}, "instructions": "Does Priya Anand's week-13 rota, exactly as shown, comply with all of WTS-4 sections 4.1, 4.2 and 4.3?", "type": "noul"}, "split": "public", "state": "KESTREL PAYMENTS — FOLLOW-THE-SUN OPERATIONS ROTA, WEEK 13/2026 (MON 23 MAR – SUN 29 MAR 2026)\nEmployee: Priya Anand, Incident Commander (L2), home office New York. Contract: Working Time Standard WTS-4 (applies to all incident commanders regardless of the office they cover).\n\nWTS-4 (extract)\n4.1 Minimum rest. Between the end of one shift and the start of the next there must be at least 11 hours of rest.\n4.2 Maximum shift length. No single shift may exceed 10 hours.\n4.3 Weekly maximum. Hours worked in a rota week must not exceed 48. A rota week runs from Monday 00:00 to Sunday 24:00 in the time zone of the employee's home office; a shift is attributed to the rota week in which it starts.\n4.4 Measurement. All durations under 4.1-4.3 are measured as actual elapsed time. Shift start and end times are recorded in the local time of the office whose queue is being covered.\n4.5 Breaks. Paid on-queue breaks are part of the shift and are not deducted.\n4.6 Standby. Standby (reachable by phone, not logged in) is not a shift and is not counted, but a call-out during standby starts a shift.\n\nRota (local time of the covered office):\nMon 23 Mar  NYC queue   08:00 – 17:00\nTue 24 Mar  NYC queue   08:00 – 17:00\nWed 25 Mar  NYC queue   08:00 – 16:00\nThu 26 Mar  NYC queue   08:00 – 10:00   (half day; afternoon off)\nFri 27 Mar  NYC queue   23:00 – Sat 28 Mar 09:00   (quarter-end batch watch)\nSat 28 Mar  —\nSun 29 Mar  BER queue   01:00 – 12:00   (covering Berlin; Berlin L2 on leave)\n\nZone reference printed on the rota export:\nAmerica/New_York — EST (UTC−5) until Sun 8 Mar 2026 02:00 local; EDT (UTC−4) from then until Sun 1 Nov 2026.\nEurope/Berlin — CET (UTC+1) until Sun 29 Mar 2026 02:00 local, when clocks are set forward to 03:00; CEST (UTC+2) from then until Sun 25 Oct 2026.\n\nRota tool summary line: \"Planned hours wk 13: 49.0\".\nPrevious week (wk 12) ended with a shift Fri 20 Mar 08:00 – 16:00 NYC; no work Sat 21 or Sun 22 Mar.\nNext shift after this rota: Tue 31 Mar 08:00 NYC queue.\nChange request CR-1181 (Berlin team lead, 18 Mar): \"Could Priya's Sunday Berlin shift run 01:00 – 12:00 local instead of 02:00 – 12:00? We need someone on the queue from 01:00 for the settlement file.\" Approved by rota planner 19 Mar; reflected above.\n"}
{"expected": "eur_692_00", "family": "temporal_numeric", "group": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-03", "id": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-03", "labels": ["eur_692_00", "eur_617_00", "eur_688_00", "eur_693_90", "eur_812_00"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 598, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Notice received 10 Feb 2028; +30 calendar days in a leap February = effective date 11 Mar 2028. Term 3 has 366 days (it contains 29 Feb 2028). Remaining days after the effective date: 12 Mar-31 Aug 2028 = 173. Refund = 1,464.00 x 173/366 = 692.00. The first six months end 29 Feb 2028, so the 11 Mar effective date incurs no EUR 75 fee (fee would give 617.00). A 365-day basis gives 693.90; a non-leap effective date of 12 Mar gives 688.00; pro-rating from the notice date gives 812.00.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "eur_617_00", "why_hard": "The effective date, the six-month fee boundary and the term length all depend on 29 Feb 2028."}, "question": {"criteria": {"eur_617_00": "Refund of EUR 617.00.", "eur_688_00": "Refund of EUR 688.00.", "eur_692_00": "Refund of EUR 692.00.", "eur_693_90": "Refund of EUR 693.90.", "eur_812_00": "Refund of EUR 812.00."}, "instructions": "What refund does Vantorre Landscaping receive under section 14 of the Terms of Service?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "ORBITAL LEDGER SOFTWARE — CANCELLATION DESK WORKSHEET\nCustomer: Vantorre Landscaping BV (account OL-55213). Plan: Ledger Pro, annual, prepaid.\n\nSubscription record\nTerm 3 (current): 1 September 2027 – 31 August 2028 (both dates inclusive).\nInvoice INV-27-09-5521 (1 Sep 2027): Ledger Pro annual, list price EUR 1,722.35; annual-prepay discount 15 % (EUR −258.35); total charged EUR 1,464.00; paid 3 Sep 2027 by SEPA direct debit.\nTerm 2 (previous): 1 September 2026 – 31 August 2027, invoice INV-26-09-4410, total charged EUR 1,389.00 (then-current price).\nSeat add-on: none.\n\nTerms of Service v7.2, section 14 (Cancellation of annual plans)\n14.1 An annual plan may be cancelled at any time by written notice. Cancellation takes effect 30 calendar days after the day on which the notice is received by Orbital Ledger; access continues until the end of the effective date.\n14.2 Refund. For the days of the term that fall after the effective date, the customer receives a refund of the amount actually charged for the term, pro-rated by calendar days: (amount charged for the term) × (remaining days after the effective date) / (number of days in the term). The result is rounded to the cent.\n14.3 Early termination fee. If the effective date falls within the first six months of the term, a fee of EUR 75.00 is deducted from the refund. The first six months of a term beginning on the 1st of a month end on the last day of the sixth month.\n14.4 No refund is made for terms that ended before the notice was received, nor for add-ons billed monthly.\n14.5 Customers on monthly plans are not refunded; their cancellation takes effect at the end of the current billing month.\n\nCorrespondence\nReceived 10 February 2028, 09:12 (support portal, ticket 88410): \"Dear Orbital team, we are moving to our parent company's ERP. Please cancel our Ledger Pro subscription. Kind regards, M. Vantorre, managing director.\" Authority check: Mr Vantorre is listed as account owner — OK.\nSent 10 February 2028: acknowledgement of notice, \"the effective date will be confirmed by the cancellation desk\".\nReceived 14 February 2028 (ticket 88410): \"Could you tell us the refund amount so we can book it in February?\"\n\nDesk notes\n- Price check: Ledger Pro list price for new customers was raised to EUR 1,810.00 on 1 January 2028 (does not change existing terms).\n- Refund channel: credit to the SEPA mandate account.\n"}
{"expected": "usd_2335_00", "family": "temporal_numeric", "group": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-04", "id": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-04", "labels": ["usd_2222_50", "usd_2303_01", "usd_2303_33", "usd_2335_00", "usd_2340_00"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 480, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Reset Date = second Business Day after Mon 28 Feb 2028 = Wed 1 Mar 2028 (Tue 29 Feb, Wed 1 Mar). Principal 250,000 for 15-31 Jan (17 days), 200,000 from 1 Feb (funds received). Actual/360: 250,000x4.20%x17/360 = 495.83; 200,000x4.20%x29/360 = 676.67 (leap Feb 1-29); 200,000x4.65%x45/360 = 1,162.50 (1 Mar-14 Apr). Total 2,335.00. Ignoring the reset gives 2,222.50; 30/360 gives 2,303.33; Actual/365 gives 2,303.01; applying the new rate from the fixing date gives 2,340.00.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "usd_2340_00", "why_hard": "Needs business-day reset logic, a mid-period principal change, a leap February and the stated Actual/360 basis."}, "question": {"criteria": {"usd_2222_50": "Interest for Interest Period 9 of USD 2,222.50.", "usd_2303_01": "Interest for Interest Period 9 of USD 2,303.01.", "usd_2303_33": "Interest for Interest Period 9 of USD 2,303.33.", "usd_2335_00": "Interest for Interest Period 9 of USD 2,335.00.", "usd_2340_00": "Interest for Interest Period 9 of USD 2,340.00."}, "instructions": "What is the interest payable for Interest Period 9 of loan TL-88-2291?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "CALDERWOOD REGIONAL BANK — COMMERCIAL LENDING: INTEREST PERIOD STATEMENT CHECK\nFacility: term loan TL-88-2291, borrower Mistral Cold Storage LLC. Currency USD.\n\nFacility agreement (extract)\n§5.1 Interest accrues daily on the principal outstanding at the start of each day, from and including the first day of an Interest Period to but excluding its last day.\n§5.2 Day count: actual number of days elapsed divided by 360.\n§5.3 Rate: the Fixed Margin Rate applicable on each day. The rate applicable from 15 January 2028 is 4.20 % p.a. Upon a rate fixing, the new rate applies from and including the Reset Date, which is the second Business Day after the fixing date. Business Day: Monday to Friday, other than a day on which banks in New York are closed.\n§5.4 Voluntary prepayment: a prepayment reduces the principal outstanding from the start of the day on which the prepaid funds are received by the Bank. Prepayments do not change the Interest Period dates.\n§5.5 Interest for each Interest Period is payable on its last day and rounded to the nearest cent.\n§5.6 Default interest (+2.00 %) applies only to overdue amounts; none outstanding.\n\nInterest Period 9: 15 January 2028 – 15 April 2028.\nPrincipal outstanding on 15 January 2028: USD 250,000.00.\nEvents in Interest Period 9:\n- Borrower instructed a voluntary prepayment of USD 50,000.00 on Monday 31 January 2028 (instruction time 16:40); funds received by the Bank Tuesday 1 February 2028.\n- Rate fixing on Monday 28 February 2028: new Fixed Margin Rate 4.65 % p.a.\n- Bank holidays (New York) in the period: Mon 17 Jan 2028, Mon 21 Feb 2028. No other closures.\n- Borrower's treasury asked on 3 March 2028 whether the interest could be computed on a 30/360 basis \"as in our previous facility TL-71-0843\"; request declined, agreement unchanged.\n\nCalendar reference: 2028 is a leap year (February has 29 days); 28 February 2028 is a Monday, 1 March 2028 a Wednesday.\n"}
{"expected": "sep_18_book_set", "family": "temporal_numeric", "group": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-06", "id": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-06", "labels": ["sep_15_office_chair", "sep_16_taxi", "sep_17_parking", "sep_18_book_set", "sep_21_client_dinner", "none_declined"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 695, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Before 15 Sep: settled 1,240 (air) + 612.40 (hotel settlement replaces the 900 hold) + 1,493.50 (1,450 + 3% foreign fee) + 1,188 = 4,533.90; the 400 partial refund restores nothing (B6); the 700 car hold is open until released at 00:00 on 19 Sep (8th day after 11 Sep). Available on 15 Sep = 6,000 - 4,533.90 - 700 = 766.10: chair 620 approved (146.10 left); 16 Sep taxi 123.60 incl. fee approved (22.50); 17 Sep parking 18 approved (4.50); 18 Sep books 240 > 4.50 declined. On 21 Sep the hold is released (704.50), so the dinner is approved. Letting the 400 partial refund restore limit, or releasing the car hold on 18 Sep (7 days), leaves the book set approved and nothing declined.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "none_declined", "why_hard": "A running available-limit simulation with pre-auth replacement, a foreign fee, a non-restoring partial refund and an 8th-day release."}, "question": {"criteria": {"none_declined": "None of the listed purchases was declined.", "sep_15_office_chair": "The 15 Sep office chair (USD 620.00) is the first declined purchase.", "sep_16_taxi": "The 16 Sep London taxi is the first declined purchase.", "sep_17_parking": "The 17 Sep parking (USD 18.00) is the first declined purchase.", "sep_18_book_set": "The 18 Sep book set (USD 240.00) is the first declined purchase.", "sep_21_client_dinner": "The 21 Sep client dinner (USD 310.00) is the first declined purchase."}, "instructions": "Under schedule B, which listed purchase on card 4471 was the first one declined?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "HARWICK & DOYLE CONSULTING — CORPORATE CARD DESK\nQuery from cardholder Leonie Marsh (card ending 4471): \"Which of my September purchases got declined? I got a decline text but deleted it.\"\n\nCard programme rules (Corporate Card Terms, schedule B)\nB1 Cycle limit: USD 6,000.00 per statement cycle. The September cycle runs 1–30 September 2026.\nB2 A purchase is approved only if the amount it would charge, including any fee charged with it, is not more than the available limit at that moment. A declined purchase has no effect on the limit.\nB3 Available limit = cycle limit − settled purchases and fees in the cycle − open authorisations + restored amounts.\nB4 Pre-authorisations (hotels, car rental): the authorised amount is held as an open authorisation. When the merchant settles, the settled amount replaces the authorised amount. An authorisation that has not settled is released at 00:00 on the 8th calendar day after the day of authorisation.\nB5 Foreign transaction fee: 3 % of the USD amount of any purchase from a merchant outside the United States, charged together with the purchase.\nB6 Refunds: a refund of the full amount of a purchase restores that amount to the available limit on its posting date. A partial refund is credited to the statement balance but does not restore available limit within the cycle.\nB7 Fees and refunds on pre-authorisations follow the settlement, not the authorisation.\n\nCard activity, September 2026 (card ending 4471), in order. Times omitted; no two purchases on the same day.\n02 Sep  Transatlantic Air (US)                  purchase  1,240.00 USD   settled\n03 Sep  Harbour Point Hotel, Boston (US)        pre-authorisation  900.00 USD\n06 Sep  Harbour Point Hotel, Boston (US)        settlement  612.40 USD\n08 Sep  EuroGrid Summit, Vienna (AT)            purchase  1,450.00 USD equivalent (charged in EUR)\n10 Sep  Transatlantic Air (US)                  refund  400.00 USD (ticket change; original fare 1,240.00, change fee retained)\n11 Sep  Sunbelt Car Rental, Austin (US)         pre-authorisation  700.00 USD  — vehicle returned 13 Sep; merchant has not settled as of 30 Sep\n14 Sep  Paxton Analytics (US)                   purchase  1,188.00 USD (annual software licence)\n15 Sep  Brightline Office Supply (US)           purchase  620.00 USD (office chair)\n16 Sep  CityCab London (GB)                     purchase  120.00 USD equivalent (charged in GBP)\n17 Sep  Metro Parking, Austin (US)              purchase  18.00 USD\n18 Sep  Folio & Quire Books (US)                purchase  240.00 USD (training book set)\n21 Sep  Marlowe Grill, Boston (US)              purchase  310.00 USD (client dinner)\n\nOther card on the same account: card ending 4417 (Leonie Marsh, travel lodge card, separate USD 2,500 limit) — no activity in September.\n"}
{"expected": "eur_498_81", "family": "temporal_numeric", "group": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-08", "id": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-08", "labels": ["eur_506_06", "eur_498_81", "eur_513_69", "eur_497_40", "eur_532_01"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 644, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "The transaction date is the folio charge date Sun 11 Oct; no rate is published that day, so Fri 9 Oct (162.40) applies, not the posting date (13 Oct) or Monday. Rooms: 26,400/162.40 = 162.56 twice (under the 170 cap), 33,000/162.40 = 203.20 capped at 170.00 per night; accommodation tax 600/162.40 = 3.69 in full; breakfast excluded. 162.56+162.56+170.00+3.69 = 498.81. Monday's rate gives 506.06, the check-in rate 497.40, a pooled 3x170 cap 513.69, no cap 532.01.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "eur_506_06", "why_hard": "The rate date needs the folio charge date plus a weekend fall-back, and the cap applies per night with tax outside it."}, "question": {"criteria": {"eur_497_40": "Reimbursable lodging amount EUR 497.40.", "eur_498_81": "Reimbursable lodging amount EUR 498.81.", "eur_506_06": "Reimbursable lodging amount EUR 506.06.", "eur_513_69": "Reimbursable lodging amount EUR 513.69.", "eur_532_01": "Reimbursable lodging amount EUR 532.01."}, "instructions": "What is the reimbursable lodging amount for the Tokyo hotel under TP-9?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "TRAVEL & EXPENSE AUDIT — CLAIM EXP-2026-10-2217\nEmployee: Jonas Feldhaus, Senior Sales Engineer, Vireon Robotics GmbH (home currency EUR).\nTrip: customer workshop, Tokyo, 8–11 October 2026. Hotel: Shiba Park Tower Hotel, Minato-ku, Tokyo.\n\nTravel Policy TP-9 (excerpt)\n7.2 Foreign-currency expenses are converted to EUR at the company reference rate (Treasury table) for the transaction date. If no reference rate was published for the transaction date, the most recent rate published before that date is used. Bank or card conversion rates are not used, even where the card statement shows a EUR amount.\n7.3 Transaction date: the date on which the merchant charged the expense, as shown on the receipt or hotel folio. The card posting date is not the transaction date.\n7.4 Conversion is made line by line; each converted line is rounded to the cent.\n8.1 Lodging is reimbursed up to the nightly cap for the city (Annex L). The cap applies to the room charge of each night separately; an amount of one night below the cap cannot be used to offset another night above it.\n8.2 Local accommodation or lodging taxes are reimbursed in full in addition to the nightly cap.\n8.3 Hotel breakfast, minibar and laundry are not reimbursed through the lodging line (breakfast is covered by the meal per diem, section 9).\n\nAnnex L (lodging caps, EUR per night, 2026): Osaka 150; Tokyo (23 wards) 170; Tokyo — Narita airport hotels 140; Yokohama 150; Seoul 165.\n\nTreasury reference rates (EUR 1 = JPY), published on Frankfurt business days:\nWed 07 Oct 2026  163.85\nThu 08 Oct 2026  163.10\nFri 09 Oct 2026  162.40\nMon 12 Oct 2026  158.90\nTue 13 Oct 2026  159.25\n\nHotel folio no. 55-10871 (all amounts JPY; folio closed and card charged at check-out)\nArrival Thu 08 Oct 2026, departure Sun 11 Oct 2026; 3 nights; charge date on folio: 11 Oct 2026.\n08 Oct  Room, superior king (incl. consumption tax)   26,400\n08 Oct  Tokyo accommodation tax                          200\n09 Oct  Room, superior king (incl. consumption tax)   26,400\n09 Oct  Tokyo accommodation tax                          200\n09 Oct  Breakfast                                       2,800\n10 Oct  Room, superior king — weekend rate            33,000\n10 Oct  Tokyo accommodation tax                          200\n10 Oct  Breakfast                                       2,800\nTotal charged to card ending 0932                     92,000\n\nCard statement: posted Tue 13 Oct 2026, JPY 92,000.00 = EUR 579.35 (card issuer rate), foreign fee EUR 10.43 (not claimable).\nEmployee's claim line: \"Hotel Tokyo, per card statement: EUR 579.35\".\n"}
{"expected": "expired_30_month_cap", "family": "temporal_numeric", "group": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-12", "id": "hard-opus-c-temporal_numeric-12", "labels": ["covered", "expired_24_month_term", "expired_30_month_cap"], "provenance": {"approx_state_tokens": 608, "author_model": "claude-opus-5", "frozen_utc": "2026-09-19T11:43:54+00:00", "label_basis": "Authored with rationale; cross-model review before any system run", "license": "MIT", "rationale": "Manufacture: ISO 2024-W12 -> Monday 18 Mar 2024; the §6 limit ends 18 Sep 2026. Delivery 5 Sep 2024 + 24 months = 5 Sep 2026, extended by 22 repair days (3-24 Nov 2025 inclusive) to 27 Sep 2026. The claim date is the portal timestamp in Amsterdam time: 02:10 UTC 19 Sep = 04:10 CEST on 19 Sep 2026 (the customer's 18 Sep is Los Angeles time). 19 Sep is within the extended 24-month period but after 18 Sep, so the non-extendable 30-month limit bars the claim.", "source": "JevBench v1.2 hard tier (original authored scenario)", "surface_answer": "covered", "why_hard": "Needs an ISO-week decode, a repair-day extension that the cap overrides, and a time-zone switch that moves the claim date across the cap by one day."}, "question": {"criteria": {"covered": "The claim is within warranty.", "expired_24_month_term": "The claim is outside warranty because the 24-month period (with any §2 extension) has ended.", "expired_30_month_cap": "The 24-month period (with extension) is still running, but the claim falls after the §6 30-month manufacture limit."}, "instructions": "How must warranty claim WD-26-30417 be classified under WS-2024?", "type": "choice"}, "split": "public", "state": "VELDHOVEN AUDIO B.V. — WARRANTY DESK (Amsterdam), CASE WD-26-30417\nProduct: Veldhoven Reference R7 active studio monitor, serial VA-R7-TL-2412-00873.\nCustomer: Hollis Street Sound LLC, Los Angeles, USA (commercial customer).\n\nWarranty Statement WS-2024 (applies to units sold from 1 January 2024)\n§1 The warranty covers defects in materials and workmanship for 24 months from the date of delivery to the first end customer.\n§2 The 24-month period is extended by the number of days a unit spent at an authorised service centre for a repair accepted under this warranty, counted from the day of receipt at the centre to the day of dispatch back to the customer, both inclusive.\n§3 A claim is made on the claim date: the date on which the fault is first reported through the Veldhoven service portal. The claim date is determined from the portal's timestamp in Amsterdam time (the portal operates on Amsterdam time for all regions).\n§4 Units sold before 1 January 2024 remain under WS-2019 (18 months from delivery, no extension).\n§5 Consumables (grille cloth, rubber feet) are excluded.\n§6 Notwithstanding §§1–2, no claim is accepted after the end of the 30th month following the date of manufacture. This limit is not extended by repair days or for any other reason.\n§7 Date of manufacture: the serial number format is VA-<model>-<plant>-<YYWW>-<sequence>, where YYWW is the ISO year and ISO week of manufacture; the date of manufacture is deemed to be the Monday of that ISO week.\n§8 Periods in months end on the corresponding calendar day of the final month.\n\nUnit history\nSold via distributor Pacific Pro Audio; delivered to Hollis Street Sound on 5 September 2024 (carrier proof of delivery).\nRepair RMA-25-11020: tweeter failure, accepted under warranty. Received at the Burbank service centre 3 November 2025; repaired 18 November; dispatched back 24 November 2025.\nSecond unit of the same pair: serial VA-R7-TL-2414-00911, delivered the same day, no repairs.\n\nNew fault report (portal ticket 7730-A): \"Left monitor (…-2412-00873) — woofer rattles at low volume.\" Portal timestamp: 2026-09-19 02:10:44 UTC.\nCustomer follow-up e-mail: \"We logged this on Friday afternoon, September 18, well inside your warranty — the repair last year extended it to late September.\"\nCalendar reference: ISO week 12 of 2024 runs Monday 18 March – Sunday 24 March 2024. Amsterdam is on CEST (UTC+2) in September 2026; Los Angeles on PDT (UTC−7).\n"}
